+      "prefix": "<!-- BEGIN CURRENT PUBLIC RECONCILIATION 2026-11-04 -->\n# Later source reports — November 4 reconciliation\n\nThe earlier 60-body cohort is preserved below, followed by **65 additional complete research report bodies**. Private source locations are replaced with explicit citation limits. Historical status labels remain dated; this publication does not authenticate the underlying events or complete the original tests.\n\n<!-- END CURRENT PUBLIC RECONCILIATION 2026-11-04 -->\n\n",
+      "suffix": "\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT RECONCILIATION 2026-11-04 -->\n\n## November 4 qualified report supplements\n\nThese 65 later research-report bodies were not frozen in the preceding catalogue. They include corrections and failed checks. They overlap the source register and earlier reports and are not independent evidence. Current publication status was not rechecked; preserve each owner’s dated claim and limitations. Full original report bytes are separately pinned.\n\n<a id=\"supplement-c607864b037b456d\"></a>\n### ASCE letter: conflicting dates confirmed in the reproduced page images\n\n**Source unit:** `asce-letter-date-facsimiles-2026-11-02` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-3c5800826f1fe9301e93).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY c607864b037b456d88d051c32e7890796a32f364c09c4aca9cf92e1354fc0ab2 -->\n# ASCE letter: conflicting dates confirmed in the reproduced page images\n\nNovember 2, 2026. Existing STL01/STL04, SPT01–SPT08/SR01, B188/H005 and AUD011/AUD016 remain open. This supplement completes a previously deferred page-image check. It does not recover native correspondence or decide which date is correct.\n\n## What the pages show\n\n| Record | Exact visible date | Location |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Letter first page | **November 15, 2001** | Printed 163, below ASCE letterhead and above the addressee. [Page image source](https://books.google.com/books?id=R1GrCiT1US8C&pg=PA163) |\n| Continuation 2 of 3 | **September 15, 2001** | Printed 164, upper-left continuation header. [Page image source](https://books.google.com/books?id=R1GrCiT1US8C&pg=PA164) |\n| Continuation 3 of 3 | **September 15, 2001** | Printed 165, upper-left continuation header. [Page image source](https://books.google.com/books?id=R1GrCiT1US8C&pg=PA165) |\n\nThese pages reproduce one ASCE letter addressed to Commerce Secretary Donald Evans. The reproduced body thanks NIST's laboratory for assistance and seeks support for a public/private research program. That context was already available in the text export; it is not a new funding or technical finding.\n\nThe [preceding study](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/e0e2c12fb5363eaf15f6ea2be2e4679d6afa79e4/investigation/broader-evidence/steel-cooperation-records-2026-11-01/OPEN-RECORDS.md) preserved these dates as unverified text-layer observations. The three newly acquired images visibly repeat them. Both representations remain preserved, without selecting a controlling value. Three pages, two representation types and repeated dates do not constitute separate letters or independent corroboration.\n\n## What this means and does not establish\n\nThe verified addition is narrow: the public reproduction presents inconsistent date fields across its first page and continuation headers. Confidence is high in that visible reading; the native letter's date and the reason for the discrepancy remain unknown.\n\nPossible causes include drafting/header reuse, different page versions or later assembly/reproduction differences. These are untested alternatives, not findings. The observations do not establish backdating, fabrication, coordination, concealment or an error in NIST's collapse calculations. An innocuous document-production issue could explain the record; native versions and transmission records are needed to test that possibility alongside the others.\n\n## Scope and next records\n\nAll three displayed page images were acquired and visually examined; the three date lines in the held text export were rechecked. [SOURCES.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-78fa9ed45f5f4f0e697a) records URLs, acquisition times, exact page/header locations, image dimensions and SHA-256 values. No complete-book review, native-letter authentication, signature analysis or independent edition comparison was performed.\n\n[TODOs](CITATIONS.md#nov04-6dc743d4bd3985a70ebc) retain native ASCE/Commerce correspondence, version history, dispatch/receipt and the congressional submission/assembly chain. The earlier steel custody/attribution, FAA correction/disposition and other original-record requirements remain open. No outreach or request was sent. The earlier reports and conflicting source bytes remain unchanged. [Checklist](CITATIONS.md#nov04-c9256527d5578a69acb2), [verification](CITATIONS.md#nov04-72439eefe733a2992576) and [addition diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-65a3792c213a02e1bae2) distinguish the performed check from unresolved historical questions.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY c607864b037b456d88d051c32e7890796a32f364c09c4aca9cf92e1354fc0ab2 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-5ee6f88b5be0e359\"></a>\n### Beverly Hills recorded approval of the WTC steel transfer\n\n**Source unit:** `bh-steel-ratification-minutes-2026-11-03` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-4af005b833a0afe36dc8).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY 5ee6f88b5be0e3590bb8f786ac7206893cfd7d0aeaf9a1edc01b8b9c69eaa2ec -->\n# Beverly Hills recorded approval of the WTC steel transfer\n\n**November 3, 2026.** The restored share made the preserved September 8, 2011 council minutes readable. They record adoption of the steel-transfer ratification as **resolution 11-R-12833**. The related consent-calendar vote lists **five ayes and no noes**. This supplies a decision record missing from our earlier agenda-packet review. Physical delivery, specimen identification and testing remain unverified.\n\nResearch identifiers: STL01–STL04; AUD011, AUD016; B188, H005; T01–T07; BH-M01–BH-M04. This is a public-source research update, not a court filing or a scientific test.\n\n## What the minutes say\n\nThe [official minutes](https://beverlyhills.granicus.com/DocumentViewer.php?file=beverlyhills_e8dee5d0768d9bb4bc2d443199135f32.pdf&view=1) identify the September 8, 2011 **Adjourned Regular Meeting**. The archive associates it with the Formal Meeting, clip **2444**; the same-date Study Session has a different clip, **2442**. Physical page 1 lists all five council members present, with none absent. Its separate **F-1** document marker is not the steel item's number.\n\n| Exact source location | Recorded observation | Limit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [Physical page 5, opening consent motion](https://beverlyhills.granicus.com/DocumentViewer.php?file=beverlyhills_e8dee5d0768d9bb4bc2d443199135f32.pdf&view=1#page=5) | Gold moved, Bosse seconded: remove **F-8** for a separate vote; remove **F-14I and F-14J** for discussion; approve all consent-agenda items **except F-8** by reference. | Preserve the separate exception and discussion wording. F-7 is not named as removed. The final characters in F-14I and F-14J are letters. |\n| [Physical page 6, item F-7 and disposition](https://beverlyhills.granicus.com/DocumentViewer.php?file=beverlyhills_e8dee5d0768d9bb4bc2d443199135f32.pdf&view=1#page=6) | Ratification of the Port Authority agreement transferring a WTC steel artifact for the Beverly Hills 9/11 Memorial Garden. The disposition reads **“ITEM F-7: ADOPTED RES# 11-R-12833.”** | The comment describes future display at the garden. It does not establish shipment, arrival time or physical receipt. |\n| [Physical page 10, “Vote on Consent Calendar”](https://beverlyhills.granicus.com/DocumentViewer.php?file=beverlyhills_e8dee5d0768d9bb4bc2d443199135f32.pdf&view=1#page=10) | Ayes: **Gold, Bosse, Mirisch, Brien, Brucker**. Noes: **None**. **CARRIED**. | Association with F-7 depends on the page 5 motion's scope and page 6 disposition, not the vote count alone. |\n| Physical pages 6–7, separate F-8 motion and vote | F-8 has its own motion, vote and agreement disposition. | This different item is not counted as a second steel approval. Its similarly unanimous vote is not substituted for the consent-calendar vote. |\n| [Physical page 11, approval block](https://beverlyhills.granicus.com/DocumentViewer.php?file=beverlyhills_e8dee5d0768d9bb4bc2d443199135f32.pdf&view=1#page=11) | The minutes record approval and adoption on **September 20, 2011**, with visible clerk/mayor attestation marks. | Signature marks were observed, not forensically authenticated. September 20 labels approval of the minutes; September 8 labels the meeting and item action. |\n\nThe PDF is a scan served by the official city archive. It is not a separately obtained signed resolution, native authoring file or verbatim meeting recording. The sentence concerning original signed minutes under **G. Commission Synopsis** on page 10 is not used to establish custody of this council minute or resolution: its surrounding section concerns commission records.\n\n## Preserve both versions and their different fields\n\nThe [previously held agenda packet, physical pages 4–5](https://beverlyhills.granicus.com/MetaViewer.php?clip_id=2444&meta_id=136233&view_id=11#page=4), remains unchanged. Keep these observations side by side:\n\n| Agenda's proposed-resolution copy | Later minute record |\n| --- | --- |\n| Resolution-number field stops at **11-R-**. | Item F-7 is marked adopted as **11-R-12833**. |\n| **Adopted:** field and mayor/clerk adoption-attestation lines are blank. | Minutes record the item as adopted; the minutes themselves record September 20 approval and visible attestation marks. |\n| Approval-as-to-form and Fire Chief approval marks are visible elsewhere. | These separate marks are not completed or authenticated using the minutes. |\n| Section 2 identifies steel **I-0137** and the Port Authority's WTC Artifacts Inventory Archive. | F-7 does not itself print that artifact number. Association is through the same meeting/clip and agreement subject. |\n| Section 5 directs entry of the resolution and certification into the **Book of Resolutions**. | The minutes supply a resolution number with which to seek that separate record. The entry is not reproduced in the minutes. |\n\nNo blank field was completed, source replaced or earlier assessment rewritten. The earlier report correctly recorded what the then-held packet did not verify; the newly read minutes add a separate observation. These records have different roles. Their existence does not by itself establish why a particular field was blank, authenticate every version, or demonstrate file substitution.\n\nThe earlier visual **I-0137** versus extracted **1-0137** caution remains preserved. This review does not adjudicate that reading or turn it into a new independent discrepancy. Likewise, **September 8 meeting/item action** and **September 20 minutes approval** retain their event labels; no legal defect or concealment is inferred from the dates alone.\n\n## What the finding changes\n\n**BH-M01 is complete to its bounded reading scope:** the source is accessible, matches its acquisition hash, and the pertinent motion, item, vote and approval block have been inspected. **BH-M02 advances partially:** we now have recorded adoption and a resolution number. The actual signed resolution, certification, approved attachments and version history remain unacquired.\n\nRecorded ratification is evidence against a blanket assertion that this transfer had no documented council authorization. It does not prove that all preservation decisions were sound, every condition was implemented, or other transfers were properly authorized. Agenda, minutes, agreement and copied order are related chain records, not independent measurements of the steel's origin or condition.\n\nNothing read here joins the municipal artifact to **W14A/W14B, C12a/b, FEMA Sample 1**, a retained NIST specimen, a particular tower or WTC 7. Nor does it establish dispatch, delivery, condition, later alteration, retained coupons or a completed test. The earlier copied court-order testing provision remains separate from evidence of its implementation or present legal enforceability. It is not treated as a testing right for every public requester.\n\n## Missing records remain TODOs\n\n1. **BH-M02 — signed decision:** obtain resolution **11-R-12833**, clerk certification, Book of Resolutions entry, approved attachments and amendment/version records. Compare them with both preserved copies without overwriting either.\n2. **BH-M03 — physical chain:** obtain the I-0137 application/inventory record, dispatch and bill of lading, recipient receipt, condition/installation/maintenance/alteration records, and later custody or disposition. Future-display wording is not receipt evidence.\n3. **BH-M04 — testing implementation:** obtain item-specific testing notices, responses, examination and sample-retention records, and later modifications to the copied order. A written condition is not a completed test.\n4. **STL02/STL03 — scientific identification:** obtain native inventories and marking, cutting, coupon, intake and transfer histories, with the actual building-attribution rationale. Generic shape or an artifact number does not establish the missing join.\n5. **Other original criteria:** preserve STL01/STL04 preservation/contractor-review dependencies and the FAA, telephone, recorder and materials TODOs. This municipal record does not complete any of the original eighteen substantive acceptance criteria across the five tracks.\n\nNo outreach, new request, fee, filing, physical handling or experimental test occurred. No inference about historical collapse mechanism, conspiracy, motive or NIST's scientific credibility is drawn from this vote. The strongest limit is that an administrative approval record cannot substitute for the incomplete physical and scientific chain.\n\n## Provenance and actual reading scope\n\n| Source | Acquisition time, UTC | Preserved bytes / pages | SHA-256 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Official council minutes | November 3, 2026, 03:15:05.626912; saved 03:15:06.496626 | **1,019,036 / 11** | `207802196bb1c2df80ffed5b0275e22dc58767a1c7d4df458c97b1b4bd365dba` |\n| Earlier agenda packet | October 27, 2026, 22:06:12.038318 | **369,384 / 12** | `140f527241183d46aba69b89533e115ef7a06d15b7a3c17f7d0a7355066a0b3a` |\n\nThe restored minute source was rechecked against its acquisition hash before reading. The former share outage was an access condition, not evidence of historical deletion or withholding. Different page counts describe two different documents; they do not demonstrate missing pages.\n\nAll eleven minute pages were inspected **as navigation thumbnails**. Only **physical pages 1, 5, 6, 7, 10 and 11** received the declared full-face inspection at 160 dpi. The earlier agenda's pages **4–5** were re-inspected at 160 dpi; the earlier twelve-page review was not repeated as a new acquisition. Unrelated comments/contracts and gallery photographs are outside the adopted findings. Ordinary text extraction yielded no useful target text. No new OCR, audio transcription, handwriting authentication, native-file recovery or independent/blinded reading was performed.\n\nThe publication includes [source pins](CITATIONS.md#nov04-9507c45375858a95c1c5), [observations](CITATIONS.md#nov04-ca693c7ef3ea5718f462), [source-join checks](CITATIONS.md#nov04-d62c903f42c10b3681d1), [reading scope](CITATIONS.md#nov04-0f05f4903900c6d36be2), [open TODOs](CITATIONS.md#nov04-491c1713e677a762949f), [exact locators](CITATIONS.md#nov04-57619e547ac8ac40e61b), [request checklist](CITATIONS.md#nov04-0ab8f70d625b10b638bf), [verification scope](CITATIONS.md#nov04-de86b2e6c93c5f24adb7), [bundle hashes](CITATIONS.md#nov04-27aa3678a11947b69684) and the additive [text diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-87420360905ac473f1a1). PDF locators are physical page/item/block references, not invented text-line numbers. Hashes verify preserved bytes, not historical truth. Publication checks are separate from source reading and substantive acceptance.\n\nThe public report is a redundant research copy. Original sources and earlier assessments remain preserved separately. Raw PDFs/images, signature facsimiles, personal/contact fields, private case material and local coordination are excluded from the public payload.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY 5ee6f88b5be0e3590bb8f786ac7206893cfd7d0aeaf9a1edc01b8b9c69eaa2ec -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-bb251068021b76b8\"></a>\n### Resolution 11-R-12833: signed first page located; certification and exhibits remain unverified\n\n**Source unit:** `bh-steel-resolution-face-2026-11-03` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-7c7867000f0ec208263d).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY bb251068021b76b8e2bb5c6cb46a35a6d4eb6cd8dfe3f68e22b9b8205fa68eca -->\n# Resolution 11-R-12833: signed first page located; certification and exhibits remain unverified\n\n**November 3, 2026.** The city’s official records search returned one exact resolution record for **11-R-12833**, dated **09-08-2011**. Its viewer identifies **application 115 / document 24099** and labels the record as nine pages. The complete first page was preserved as the actual JPEG response served by that viewer and read directly. It bears the resolution number, **“Adopted: September 8, 2011”**, and a signature image above **Barry Brucker, Mayor**. This advances the earlier minutes-only approval trail. It is not acquisition of the entire nine-page record, the original signed paper or a native signed PDF.\n\nResearch identifiers: **BH-M02; STL01–STL04; AUD011, AUD016; B188, H005; T01–T07.** Earlier research and source copies remain unchanged. Missing certification, approved attachment contents and physical-chain records remain TODOs.\n\n## Where the record was found\n\nThe official [Agreements, Ordinances, Resolutions page](https://beverlyhills.org/161/Agreements-Ordinances-Resolutions) links both the [city search form](https://apps.beverlyhills.org/cityclerk/searchagreementsordinancesresolutions/) and AppEnhancer. The [exact-number search result](https://apps.beverlyhills.org/cityclerk/searchagreementsordinancesresolutions/web.jsp?SEARCHTYPE=ALL&DOCQUERY=&QUERYTYPE=AND&VENDOR=&NUMBER=11-R-12833&DATE=&TYPE=&action=Search) displayed **one record**, type **RESOLUTION**, number **11-R-12833**, date **09-08-2011**. No alternate portal query was needed.\n\nThe result's link opened the official [resolution viewer query](https://aedocs.beverlyhills.org/AppEnhancer/datasources/AX-COBH_City/ISubmitQuery?DocNavBlocked=True&DispMode=11&AppName=CITY-RESOLUTIONS_ORDINANCES_AGREEMENTS&ViewMode=ResultSet&SearchAllRevisions=True&PublicQuery=False&FulltextQuery=&FulltextQueryType=And&FulltextOperation=AND&FulltextThesaurus=False&DATE=09-08-2011&DOC%20TYPE=RESOLUTION&NUMBER%20(ORD%2FAGR%2FRESO)=11-R-12833), which displayed document **24099**, page **1 / 9**. The stable search/query locators are retained; temporary query-result and export-job identifiers are excluded from the public payload. A nine-page viewer label is not verification that all nine pages were acquired or read.\n\n## What the complete first page establishes at the document level\n\n| Source location: viewer page 1, printed page -1- | Directly observed content | What remains unestablished |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Heading and title | **RESOLUTION NO. 11-R-12833**; ratification of the Port Authority agreement for a WTC steel artifact and the Beverly Hills 9/11 Memorial Garden. | Native authoring history, signature authenticity and completeness of the full archived record. |\n| Section 1 | Reports a Fire Department application to receive an artifact for display at the city memorial location. | The application itself, dispatch, delivery and receipt. |\n| Section 2 | Reports approval of the request in **2009**, describes court permission to transfer legal ownership of steel identified as **I-0137**, and says a copy of the court order and letter agreement are attached as **Exhibit A**. | Independent verification of the reported 2009 acts and the actual contents, completeness, execution or version of those attachments in this archive record. A statement that papers are attached is not inspection of the papers. |\n| Section 3 | Ratifies the letter agreement described as executed by the Fire Department on the city’s behalf. | The actual executed agreement, its signature history and physical performance. |\n| Section 4 | Records acceptance of the artifact transfer on the city’s behalf. | Shipment/arrival date, condition, custody and physical specimen identification. |\n| Section 5 | Directs the clerk to certify adoption and cause the resolution and certification to be entered in the **Book of Resolutions**. | A direction to certify is not the certification or proof that the separate book entry was made. |\n| Bottom adoption/signature block | **Adopted: September 8, 2011**; mayoral signature image above the printed name **Barry Brucker** and mayor title. | No forensic handwriting analysis, original-paper inspection, cryptographic signature validation or signature-date authentication was performed. |\n| Footer | Printed **-1-** and `B0785-0001\\1387886v1.doc`. | The printed filename is a source locator, not possession or authentication of that native Word file. |\n\nThe first page contains Sections 1–5 and the adoption/signature block; it was read as a complete face, not inferred from the initially visible upper portion. Other pages' thumbnails and page-count labels are not adopted as substantive evidence about certification, signatures or exhibit contents.\n\n## Preserve the earlier fields alongside the new ones\n\nThe earlier [agenda packet](https://beverlyhills.granicus.com/MetaViewer.php?clip_id=2444&meta_id=136233&view_id=11), physical pages 4–5, had a proposed-resolution heading ending **11-R-**, a blank **Adopted:** field and blank mayor/clerk adoption-attestation lines. Separate approval-as-to-form and Fire Chief marks were visible; it was not wholly unsigned. Those observations and original bytes remain unchanged.\n\nThe later [minutes](https://beverlyhills.granicus.com/DocumentViewer.php?file=beverlyhills_e8dee5d0768d9bb4bc2d443199135f32.pdf&view=1), physical page 6, record **F-7 adopted as 11-R-12833**. Their consent-calendar vote is on page 10; the minutes record their own approval on **September 20, 2011** on page 11. The [preceding published review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/4ac0f984339daabcf92b2ffe71c046d0d3d0dff5/investigation/broader-evidence/bh-steel-ratification-minutes-2026-11-03/README.md) preserves the motion, exceptions, separate F-8 vote and reading limits.\n\nThe newly held resolution face instead prints the full number, **September 8 adoption**, and the mayoral signature image. Keep all three records and their labels. No earlier blank is completed, source replaced or cause of a difference adjudicated. September 8 resolution adoption and September 20 approval of the minutes retain their different event roles. No legal defect, concealment, backdating or substitution is inferred from those dates or fields.\n\nThe agenda is a **12-page PDF**, the minutes an **11-page PDF**, and the new archive viewer labels its record **nine pages**. They are different record representations. This comparison does not establish missing, removed or additional attachment pages. No complete page-by-page or byte-identity comparison of the nine-page record has been performed. The earlier visual **I-0137** versus extracted **1-0137** caution remains preserved; the new first page visually reads **I-0137**, without adjudicating the earlier extraction conflict.\n\n## Acquisition limits and the access boundary\n\nThe viewer’s all-pages PDF export did not produce a captured download. One attempt to save its displayed completed-export link also timed out. A single unauthenticated retrieval of that exact save link returned **HTTP 403**. During subsequent visible page navigation, an access check returned **403** with a login redirect. No credentials were entered, denied route retried after that boundary, security flag changed or authentication bypass attempted.\n\nThe already served **HTTP 200 image/jpeg** response for document **24099 / page 1 / version 1** was preserved from the browser’s incoming response buffer. This did not issue a new request to the denied export or lock endpoints. The image contains the full first page at **1700 × 2200 pixels**, **464,622 bytes**, SHA-256 **`2c703a9c32432650f1593d9d93e6d72d5a398c66d99c7cf5e199358639c77c6d`**. It is a rendered archive image, not an acquired PDF or authenticated native scan master. The hash identifies those saved response bytes, not the truth of every statement in them.\n\nThe official navigation and exact-number index HTML entities were also preserved with URLs, acquisition UTC, byte counts and hashes. Their original-source line/block locators and the report’s own line references are recorded separately. No invented OCR lines are assigned to the image. The raw entities, signature image, response internals and temporary acquisition details remain outside the public payload.\n\nOnly the complete first resolution face was substantively read in this batch. Pages **2–9** are **unread/unacquired as full faces**. The agenda and minute comparisons reuse the preceding preserved readings and hashes; no fresh complete reading of those PDFs or their attachments is claimed. No new OCR, audio transcription, native-file recovery, independent/blinded review, steel handling or physical test occurred. Temporary browser layout and diagnostic settings were restored.\n\n## TODOs and next discriminating records\n\n1. **BH-M02-CERT — clerk certification:** obtain and read the actual attestation/certification face or separate certification record. Preserve its wording, signature/seal observations, date, vote and source relationship. Section 5 alone does not satisfy this.\n2. **BH-M02-ATT — approved attachments:** obtain all remaining full faces of document 24099 or an approved complete export, identify the actual Exhibit A boundary, and compare the letter agreement/court order with the preserved agenda copies. Check signatures, object identifiers, conditions, schedules and amendments; retain any differences. No claim about these unread pages’ contents is made now.\n3. **BH-M02-BOOK/VERSION — book entry and source versions:** obtain the Book of Resolutions entry, complete approved record, posting/version history and any native or certified copy that the records holder lawfully makes available. The current capture is one rendered page.\n4. **BH-M03 — physical chain:** application/inventory, dispatch/bill of lading, receipt, condition, installation/alteration and subsequent custody/disposition remain missing.\n5. **BH-M04 — testing:** actual testing notices, responses, examinations, retained samples and later order modifications remain missing. Neither a referenced attachment nor authorization language is evidence that testing occurred.\n\nThis is a **partial BH-M02 advance**, not completion of that acquisition task or any of the original eighteen substantive criteria across the five tracks. A further official record bearing a signature is evidence against a blanket claim of no recorded approval, but related agenda/minutes/resolution statements are not independent scientific corroboration. The paper trail does not identify a NIST/FEMA specimen, establish a particular building of origin, validate preservation adequacy or show a conspiracy, motive or historical collapse mechanism.\n\n[Source pins](CITATIONS.md#nov04-c309938407e235ab930f), [observations](CITATIONS.md#nov04-994aa3b9fab7f58da7e4), [preserved fields](CITATIONS.md#nov04-783cc7b6543c46e4126e), [reading scope](CITATIONS.md#nov04-77d61db824dabd52b232), [TODOs](CITATIONS.md#nov04-997b845b5d2af8e8b684), [locators](CITATIONS.md#nov04-d6edb99491cfd65fffdb), [checklist](CITATIONS.md#nov04-e473200484f90abbcbb5), [verification](CITATIONS.md#nov04-77c66df09c96bc80cdfb), [bundle hashes](CITATIONS.md#nov04-7893f22fc25be2bce1e5) and the additive [revision diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-4f25e4ac1a0f66b410e4) accompany the redundant public research copy. Publication verification is separate from source authentication and substantive acceptance. No outreach, new request, fee, filing, private-repository push, overwrite or deletion occurred.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY bb251068021b76b8e2bb5c6cb46a35a6d4eb6cd8dfe3f68e22b9b8205fa68eca -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-5c5adacb1d0184a2\"></a>\n### FAA collection certification and reproduction records: what was asked, copied and actually evidenced\n\n**Source unit:** `faa-a90-certification-worklogs-2026-11-01` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-5e994da6d8b22b7c8cdd).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY 5c5adacb1d0184a24abc84f45e85dcae75a2423c15cc97f5905b7e17ab2b38aa -->\n# FAA collection certification and reproduction records: what was asked, copied and actually evidenced\n\nNovember 1, 2026. B129 / PD01–PD04 / FAA01–FAA04. Three formerly catalog-only FAA records were acquired from their actual NARA download links and read in full. They expose specific collection instructions, reported production quantities and a historical procedure excerpt. **They do not establish complete collection, successful delivery, suppressed speech or a cover-up.** Earlier sources and conflicting descriptions remain unchanged.\n\n## Sources and actual scope\n\n| Official released scan | Access and response entity | SHA-256 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [A90-74](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialive/99/9114/34911499/content/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-e-other/5-A90-74-Facility-Certification-REDACT-SEALED.pdf) | 2026-11-01T20:46:32.019719+00:00; HTTP 200; 917,957 bytes; 8 pages | `a76efe92ee6d4c6932f92b46ae3cc35c3ce59926076439401886d146258b328b` |\n| [A90-98](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-Miscellaneous/e-Other/5-A90-98-Email-on-9-11-Commission-Data-Collection.pdf) | 2026-11-01T20:46:32.025903+00:00; HTTP 200; 24,765 bytes; 1 pages | `3a8fc6ee8fc351cbcd32ea4e66da81b37b52737fe720c52fe320528e1f6c8b42` |\n| [A90-99](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialive/5/9115/34911505/content/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-e-other/5-A90-99-Reproducing-Documentation-Info-REDACT-SEALED.pdf) | 2026-11-01T20:46:32.025938+00:00; HTTP 200; 1,427,431 bytes; 6 pages | `315028f4a0b1f89a2b705c99aa857188b0d49a0de25cd38d8691411d8497d28f` |\n\nPhysical page references below are one-based. All **15** new page faces were rendered at 2600 pixels and inspected. [SOURCE-PINS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-b703d8d8c2c00c0f2580) pins original response entities, catalog snapshots, reading rasters and reused context. Raw PDFs and images remain preserved separately; this public derivative excludes personal names, signatures, contact details, individual pay data and redacted content. These are official released scans, not independently authenticated native emails or original signatures. A hash identifies examined bytes, not historical truth.\n\n## Certification concerns asking employees, not certifying complete production\n\n[A90-74 physical page 2](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialive/99/9114/34911499/content/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-e-other/5-A90-74-Facility-Certification-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=2), dated **October 15, 2003**, goes from Manager, Boston TRACON, A90-1 to Director of Air Traffic, AAT-1. It certifies that employees who were at the facility on September 11 had been asked for relevant documentation. The examples include government records, emails, radar/audiotapes and personal notes.\n\nThat is affirmative evidence of a reported collection inquiry. The actual sentence does **not** certify that every employee answered, that each answer was accurate, that all responsive records were received, or that the Commission received every item. Treating it as an independently verified completeness declaration would overstate it. Conversely, its limited wording is not evidence that someone refused or concealed records.\n\n[Physical pages 3–4](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialive/99/9114/34911499/content/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-e-other/5-A90-74-Facility-Certification-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=3) attach a sign-on log with a printed window of **9/10/01 10:00:00 PM–9/11/01 10:00:00 PM** and headers **9/12/01 7:27:58 AM** and **7:28:00 AM**. [Physical pages 5–6](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialive/99/9114/34911499/content/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-e-other/5-A90-74-Facility-Certification-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=5) contain two presentations of questions covering records received/generated from **September 11 at midnight through 11:59 pm**. These are differently described scopes and clocks; do not silently convert one into the other, identify header meaning without system records, or declare the roster incomplete. The source of each list, timezone, scope instructions and response crosswalk remain needed.\n\nThe question sheet distinguishes **government records**, which employees are described as **required** to provide, from **personal notes**, which they are **strongly encouraged** to provide. The deadline blanks are unfilled in the inspected form. That distinction is a concrete completeness question: ask what records were classified in each category and what responses were retained. It is not proof personal notes existed or were intentionally excluded. The manager's certification includes personal notes among the requested material.\n\n[Physical page 8](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialive/99/9114/34911499/content/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-e-other/5-A90-74-Facility-Certification-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=8) is a staffing sheet with handwritten marks, including no/none entries and checks. Its annotation key, question, authorship and row-to-response meaning are not established here. Do not turn those marks into a response percentage or infer an employee had no relevant knowledge. Names and signatures are not reproduced in this derivative.\n\nThe fax cover on [physical page 1](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialive/99/9114/34911499/content/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-e-other/5-A90-74-Facility-Certification-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=1) is dated **10/15/03** and states **four pages including the cover**. The released PDF has **eight physical pages**, including alternate/enlarged presentations of signed material and a staffing sheet; page 5 expressly announces an alternate presentation on the following page. Preserve both counts. The current eight-page archive object is not necessarily the original four-page fax job. No machine transmission report, exact original enclosure map or recipient acknowledgment was acquired. This difference alone establishes neither a missing historical enclosure nor four additional records sent.\n\n## The collection email separates cover documents from data and tracks staff time\n\n[A90-98 page 1](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-Miscellaneous/e-Other/5-A90-98-Email-on-9-11-Commission-Data-Collection.pdf#page=1) is a printed email dated **10/10/2003 11:26 AM**, subject **Tapes, Memos, Certification Letters   Track time**. It asks for copies of the memo, index or letter as data are sent onward, and directs certifications to **AAT-20**, referring to a teleconference.\n\nPreserve the exact sentence **“I/we do not what the data.”** It is printed that way. Reading what as want is plausible, but it has not been authenticated as an intended correction. The surrounding request clearly asks for copies of cover documents. That makes a separate administrative-document trail a useful retrieval target; it does not prove the sender prohibited preservation of data or that data were not delivered to another recipient.\n\nThe final paragraph requests weekly accounting of **time expended at each facility**, with an early-next-week/midday-Wednesday reporting reference. This is personnel-effort tracking, not a timeline of recorded September 11 events. The scan has no native mail headers, actual attachment identifiers or item-level delivery acknowledgment. Do not use its printed sent label alone to authenticate receipt or identify an entire downstream packet.\n\n## Production report: 30 reported staff hours, about 1,000 pages and two tapes\n\n[A90-99 physical pages 1–2](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialive/5/9115/34911505/content/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-e-other/5-A90-99-Reproducing-Documentation-Info-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=1) show two presentations of an email dated **10/10/2003 01:58 PM**, subject **Time**. Page 1 includes overlaid handwritten work notes; its presentation notice points to page 2. The typed report and repeated clean presentation are one underlying account, not two independent witnesses.\n\n| Printed category | Stated people | Printed hours |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| CPC | 4 | 12 |\n| MSS-1 | 2 | 8 |\n| MSS-3 | 1 | 7 |\n| MSS-4 | 1 | 3 |\n\nThe four displayed hour figures sum to **30**, matching the stated total. That is an arithmetic check of the printed report, not verification of payroll, actual effort or production completeness. The text reports **approximately 1,000 pages** and **two tapes of rerecordings**. Neither the approximate page count nor the labor hours measures the duration or completeness of audio.\n\nA postscript separately describes one MSS-1 spending **six hours** on today's exercise. Its inclusion in, or addition to, the stated 30 is not explicit. Preserve both; do not report a verified 36-hour total. The handwritten work/rate notes on pages 1 and 3 remain preserved in the source, but are not transcribed here or used to reconstruct individual compensation.\n\n### Two tapes versus the separate landline attachment list\n\nThe [earlier preserved A90-71 memorandum](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/665d6502ab844e1eb15e83b6403205a6f896936a/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-a90-landline-delivery-2026-11-01/README.md) names **TMU: one tape; OSIC: two tapes; OS: no visible numeral**. Its two explicit counts already sum to three, plus an unspecified OS count; singular tape makes four overall plausible, still an inference. A90-99's typed report says two.\n\nKeep these quantities and their source locations side by side. The reports do not identify the same output set through barcodes, enclosure lists, channels, dates of production or copy-generation records. Same-day proximity is insufficient to join them. Different batches, scopes or reporting cutoffs are untested possibilities; so is a reporting error. We cannot infer that two tapes disappeared, choose a controlling number, or call the mismatch resolved. Native manifests, preparation logs and dispatch/receipt records would discriminate.\n\n## Historical procedure excerpt supplies specific preservation and copying targets\n\n[A90-99 physical pages 4–6](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialive/5/9115/34911505/content/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-e-other/5-A90-99-Reproducing-Documentation-Info-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=4) include an FAA **7210.3R** extract, with visible date **2/24/00**, section 8 and printed pages **4-8-1/4-8-2**. Page 4 has an overlaid preparation/review note and a presentation notice; page 5 is the alternate, unobscured presentation; page 6 continues the text. Both presentations stay preserved. No hidden text or redaction was recovered.\n\n| Exact provision | What the excerpt describes | Limit on applying it here |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 4-8-1(a), page 5, upper left | Direct copying from an original with prior **ATD and AAT-20 approval**; qualified FAA employee retains custody and attends copying; continuous recording, minimizing original starts/stops to the maximum extent possible | Obtain the applicable version, approvals, request, copying method and original/copy identifiers before asserting that this provision governed a particular Commission derivative |\n| 4-8-1(b), page 5, left middle | Certified rerecording of portions copied; facility retention under applicable criteria; original returned to service | A general provision is not a source-specific disposition record or proof any September 11 original was overwritten |\n| 4-8-2, pages 5–6 | Preservation requests trigger removal from service and a warning label; written 20-day notice and a conditional additional 10-day notice before the described return-to-service outcome | Requires actual request, notices, approval/release, other holds and applicable accident-retention criteria; not an automatic 30-day destruction rule |\n| 4-8-3, page 5, upper right | Printed reductions of computer data carry a disclaimer distinguishing internal processing from the exact control-position display | Need native data, configuration, processing and display reconstruction; a printed table alone cannot authenticate an exact controller-screen reproduction |\n| 4-8-4, pages 5–6 | Separate copy/media/computer-cost provisions | Historical examples are not a current fee quote, Commission invoice or authority to incur fees |\n\nThe computer-data disclaimer reads: **“This document is derived from computer magnetic recordings of internal computer processing. It is not an exact representation of the control position display.”** The excerpt therefore supplies a specific reason to keep native data, processed output and a reconstructed display distinct. It does not establish an actual display error or deceptive reconstruction.\n\n### Continuous direct copies beside disclosed skip-silence processing\n\nThe direct-copy provision's continuity language belongs beside A90-71's explicit disclosure that skip silence shortened the landline rerecordings. Those statements raise a **specific applicability and copy-generation question**, not an established violation. The rule text has a specified direct-request copying context; A90-71 reports a Commission-related output. The full applicable order and amendments, special instructions, approvals and identities of source/output copies remain unjoined. Do not erase either statement or assume the provision either necessarily applied or necessarily did not apply.\n\nThe strongest objection to an adverse reading is that a continuous preserved original and a shortened listening copy could serve different purposes under different authorized instructions. That remains untested; the excerpt does not prove such an arrangement existed. Obtain both generations and their instructions, compare surviving speech and time anchors, and report actual differences. No native audio comparison was possible or performed in this unit.\n\n## What advanced and what remains open\n\nThe new records improve the collection/administrative and copying-policy map. None is the actual corrected **May 2003 QFR**, its delivery receipt, a signed action/no-action determination, or the later OIG recommendation response/implementation/closure. The two recommendations on report page 12 remain distinct from those continuing on page 13. Those FAA outcomes remain TODOs rather than being replaced by these 2003 records.\n\n[OPEN-TODOS.md](CITATIONS.md#nov04-5f55331972c356eadb9d) lists the original employee-response crosswalk, instructions/teleconference record, native email, output manifests, approval/custody/hold/notices and original-versus-rerecording tests. [OBSERVATIONS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-11cdbeb5d57314b38f05) keeps source wording, quantity differences and limitations explicit. All five work-plan tracks and eighteen substantive criteria remain open or partial. Existing Keyton/zero-duration mirrors remain acquired/inspected; canonical provenance, call/routing joins and native records remain open. Previously performed recorder tests retain their performed status. No duplicate recorder/materials experiment or independent-audit adoption is claimed.\n\n[verify.py](CITATIONS.md#nov04-902564a2764e83334319) reproduces the declared source-byte checks, page/count distinctions and printed-hour sum. It does not verify actual production, speech retention, historical clocks, collection completeness, delivery, procedural compliance or historical intent. [CHECKLIST.md](CITATIONS.md#nov04-388b01aab96d9ff372aa) and [REVISION.diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-64e2ea401b8e51716fbe) preserve accountability and the exact new text. Original scans, alternate presentations, previous reports and conflicting observations remain unchanged. No outreach, request, fee, mailbox/private-case use, filing, deletion, private push or history rewrite occurred.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY 5c5adacb1d0184a24abc84f45e85dcae75a2423c15cc97f5905b7e17ab2b38aa -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-2f856f7d2721b703\"></a>\n### A90 landline rerecordings: disclosed silence removal, differing time scopes and an index without receipts\n\n**Source unit:** `faa-a90-landline-delivery-2026-11-01` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-826efb35c7ff02cd8ab0).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY 2f856f7d2721b703074c480bb6c603658873be00af0ba439408035ba3a442f24 -->\n# A90 landline rerecordings: disclosed silence removal, differing time scopes and an index without receipts\n\nNovember 1, 2026. Existing B129 / PD01–PD04 / FAA01–FAA04. Two previously catalog-only public records were acquired and read in full. This advances the source/copy and delivery-record inquiry; native audio, correction/receipt and later administrative outcomes remain open.\n\n**The October 10, 2003 memorandum explicitly reports using “skip silence” to shorten rerecordings.** Its opening paragraph describes `0800–1200 local`, while the certification describes source data received `1200–1605 UTC`. Those stated intervals span four hours and four hours five minutes. Preserve both descriptions and their distinct labels. No actual audio gap, suppressed speech, elapsed-clock error or amount of removed material has been measured.\n\nThe associated four-page index supplies useful tab names. **It does not supply a delivery acknowledgment or identify each recording.** Its AAL11 and UAL175 tab letters differ, so joining records by letter alone would produce wrong associations. Repeated content labels also must not be silently deduplicated or counted as independent corroboration.\n\n## Sources and reading scope\n\n| Official released source | Acquisition, entity and SHA-256 | Actually read |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [A90-71 / NARA7599620](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-Miscellaneous/e-Other/5-A90-71-Memo-with-Re-Recordings.pdf) | 2026-11-01T20:30:50.234004+00:00; HTTP200; 40,254 bytes; `1a744e43b7a36e21cf01cd572acb18c73cc89a133fc5357604da0393fcdf1a66` | All1physical page, including scope, processing/certification and attachment footer |\n| [A90-87 / NARA7599635](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-Miscellaneous/e-Other/5-A90-87-Index-of-Data-Provided-9-11-Commission.pdf) | 2026-11-01T20:30:50.237343+00:00; HTTP200; 101,936 bytes; `16c09e7d0b3d404d362d047e95c82773cf2dcc1aacedc6b19b53046ac602b1e9` | All4physical pages; all90visible table rows transcribed |\n\nDownload URLs came from the actual public catalog links. Each response entity and acquisition receipt is preserved unchanged, separately from research/public derivatives. These are official released scans, not independently authenticated native originals or signature/custody chains. The hash pins the bytes examined; it does not establish historical truth.\n\nFive whole-page 2600-pixel images were read. Physical page numbers below are one-based. A90-87 physical1–2 are printed1of2/2of2; physical3 and4 are separate flight indexes each printed1of1. [SOURCE-PINS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-77426c12e887f8371e0d) records the exact inputs. [INDEX-ROWS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-1a6d2428b40c7b485d77) preserves all row labels and duplicates; only layout whitespace is simplified. No new OCR, hidden-layer extraction, redaction recovery, audio acquisition/listening/decoding, source editing or native clock fit occurred. Prior [A90-67/report context](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/73cccf17d825861ba62161cd631454446bed550c/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-a90-original-copy-trail-2026-11-01/README.md) is reused, not a new acquisition or independent corroboration.\n\n## What A90-71 actually reports\n\n[A90-71 physical1](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-Miscellaneous/e-Other/5-A90-71-Memo-with-Re-Recordings.pdf#page=1), header and four body/footer locations:\n\n| Location | Preserved assertion | What remains unverified |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Header/from/to | October10,2003; Manager,BostonTRACON,A90-1 → Manager,Evaluations and Investigation Staff,AAT-20 | Actual dispatch and recipient acknowledgment |\n| Opening scope paragraph | Certified rerecordings attached from all landlines in TMU, OSIC and OS positions, September11,2001, `0800 to1200 local` | Actual channels, contents, enclosure completeness and which local-clock convention applies |\n| Processing paragraph | “skip silence” was used; the memo describes skipping periods where nothing was said and compressing tape length | Actual settings, thresholds, time announcements and whether every relevant utterance survived processing |\n| Certification paragraph | Data derived from Digital Voice Recording System data received September11,2001, `1200 UTC`–`1605 UTC` | Native export identity, source/date/timecode/coverage/correction history |\n| Attachments footer | `TMU–1 tape`; `OSIC–2 tapes`; `OS–tape`, with no numeral visible for OS | A complete numeric attachment total and actual items received |\n\nThe two explicit numerals sum to **three**, plus an OS line with no visible numeric count. That is **not a verified three-tape total**. The singular word tape makes one OS tape, and therefore four overall, a plausible reading; it remains an inference rather than an explicit complete numeric manifest or proof of receipt. Three named position categories are not necessarily three channels. The one-page PDF contains the memorandum; no audio enclosure was acquired in this batch. That does not prove an enclosure was missing from the actual dispatch or from another released item.\n\nThe disclosed processing matters for reconstruction: playback elapsed time of a silence-shortened derivative cannot simply be treated as elapsed historical wall-clock time. That methodological implication is conditional on identifying an actual file as a descendant of these rerecordings. We have not identified or tested such a file here. The memo's explanation is affirmative contrary evidence to treating every shortened pause as covert splicing; it also does not independently prove that only silence was removed or that every utterance was preserved.\n\n**Required discriminating records/tests:** continuous original/channel exports, each certified-copy identifier, preparation/export logs and silence settings, retained time announcements/timecode, custody and actual enclosures. A later comparison should map each surviving speech interval to the original, report skipped intervals and uncertainty, and anchor clocks independently. No such experiment has been performed in this unit.\n\n## Time descriptions: preserve the five-minute span difference\n\nBoth descriptions refer to September11,2001, but the text labels their clocks and scopes differently:\n\n| Description | Printed endpoints and clock label | Arithmetic within that description |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Opening paragraph's attached-copy scope | `0800`–`1200`, **local** | 240 minutes |\n| Certification's source-data-received scope | `1200`–`1605`, **UTC** | 245 minutes |\n\nThe difference between those stated spans is **five minutes**. No timezone assumption is needed to subtract endpoints within each same-day description. If one hypothesizes that both pairs describe exactly the same boundaries under one constant clock offset, the starts require a numeric offset of240minutes and the ends245minutes. That conditional hypothesis does not make both endpoint pairs exactly equal. It does **not** measure clock drift, a five-minute gap, the silence-removal amount, or a concealed interval.\n\nUntested possibilities include a nominal requested window versus a longer actual source-data boundary, extra lead-in/out, drafting error, or genuinely different described coverage. None is adopted. Native copy/export records, contemporaneous instructions and the recordings could distinguish them. Do not relabel local as a specific timezone, substitute a preferred endpoint, normalize1605to1600, or apply an offset to other evidence.\n\n## Comparison with October8A90-67\n\n[The held October8memorandum](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-A90-67-Memo-Certifying-Re-recording.pdf#page=1) reports a certified ID/SL rerecording for `1209–1228 UTC`; broader DATs held at BostonTRACON but then unreproducible due to equipment problems; and a certification based on `DVRS` data received. October10A90-71 names **TMU/OSIC/OS landlines**, a different scope, and spells out **Digital Voice Recording System** as its source.\n\nThese are two dated source accounts. Same facility, two-day proximity, similar signatures or the shared acronym do not prove that the same original was used, the DAT problem was repaired, or the October8statement was false. Conversely, the later reported ability to make some rerecordings cannot establish that all source media were readable. Both assertions and their distinctions remain; copy-generation history, equipment records and native identifiers are missing. The earlier source and published assessment remain unchanged.\n\n## What the four-page A90-87 index supplies\n\n[Physical1–2](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-Miscellaneous/e-Other/5-A90-87-Index-of-Data-Provided-9-11-Commission.pdf#page=1): BostonTRACON(A90), numbered **TAB1–65**, with65visible rows. Physical1has1–36;physical2has37–65. It includes advisories, directives, GENOT/NOTAM entries, logs, procedures, security, press and stress-related material. These are index labels; no underlying item contents were acquired from a title alone.\n\n[Physical3](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-Miscellaneous/e-Other/5-A90-87-Index-of-Data-Provided-9-11-Commission.pdf#page=3): BostonTRACON AccidentPackage–**AAL11**, **A–M**,13category rows. [Physical4](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-Miscellaneous/e-Other/5-A90-87-Index-of-Data-Provided-9-11-Commission.pdf#page=4): BostonTRACON **UAL175**, **A–L**,12category rows. This totals **90index rows across three scopes**, not90documents, tapes, pages, independent pieces of evidence or successfully delivered items. TABnumbers are a package-organizing namespace, not the NARA folder suffix in A90-87.\n\n| Category or letter | AAL11 physical3 | UAL175 physical4 | Join implication |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| H | ATCSCC East Area Log | FOIA Freedom of Information Act | Same letter has different content |\n| I | Fax Transmission Verification Reports | Headquarters Guidance and Communications | Same letter has different content |\n| Headquarters Guidance and Communications | J | I | Same category has different letters |\n| Flight Progress Strips | K | J | Same category has different letters |\n| Certified Re-recordings | L | K | Same category has different letters |\n| RADAR Data | M | L | Same category has different letters |\n\nThe two flight indexes share11literal category labels. AAL11 additionally lists ATCSCCEastAreaLog and FaxTransmissionVerificationReports; UAL175 additionally lists FOIAFreedomofInformationAct. That is a bounded difference in index descriptions, not proof one investigation lacked other records or one packet was defective. A listed fax-verification category is a concrete retrieval lead, not itself a retrieved transmission report or proof of these particular audio deliveries.\n\nEight content labels recur within the65numbered rows, which have55distinct literal labels after layout-space transcription. Examples: `Genot1/36` at10/13, `NOTAM1/0989` at33/35/36/39, and `ORDER7110.65M` at44/46. Keep each row and tab. The same title could refer to copies, acknowledgments, distinct versions or different enclosures; those are untested possibilities. No item bytes have been joined or deduplicated. Source spelling such as `Procudures General` at57 remains in the row data; it is not a substantive suspicious finding.\n\n**What the index does not prove:** the four pages have no signed recipient acknowledgment, delivery date, tape barcode/serial/hash, copy-to-original crosswalk, per-recording channel/time list or enclosure receipt. Its catalog title says data provided, but a catalog description cannot independently certify each item was sent and received. Nor does the absence of those fields on an index establish that proof of delivery never existed. This source improves package navigation while leaving the receipt and native-content tests open.\n\n## Outstanding inputs and publication limits\n\n[OPEN-TODOS.md](CITATIONS.md#nov04-782a792a9db058dc7a04) preserves actual audio/copy manifests, blank OS count, source/copy boundary definitions, silence settings, original-to-rerecording joins, index-to-item mapping, signed delivery/receipt and applicable procedures as distinct dependencies. A90-74/98/99remain catalog-only targets from the earlier review. Available public work can continue there or in the telephone and steel original-record queues without rerunning these completed five page readings.\n\nThe actual corrected May2003QFR and acknowledgment, signed action/no-action determination, and separate OIGrecommendation response/implementation/closure remain unacquired. AAT-20routing, a2003audio memorandum, and an index do not substitute for those outcomes. All5work-plan tracks/18substantive criteria remain open/partial, with technical evaluation and independent-audit ownership unchanged. Existing Keyton/zero-duration mirrors and completed decoder tests retain their actual acquired/performed status; native provenance/routing/configuration/clock questions remain open.\n\n[OBSERVATIONS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-5e4227c87d3fb849b73f) separates source reports, arithmetic, interpretations and unperformed tests. [verify.py](CITATIONS.md#nov04-54ea0c2cb233e9d80024) checks declared bytes and reproduces the endpoint/row-label accounting; that does not establish native authenticity, speech retention, historical cause or acceptance of the substantive goal. [CHECKLIST.md](CITATIONS.md#nov04-4203e6e46897bcfda6e5) and [REVISION.diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-ac6ed22b1e0aa9250dc9) preserve requirements and exact new text. Original sources and prior publications remain unchanged. No outreach, fee, request, mailbox/private-case use, litigation edit, deletion, private push or history rewrite occurred.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY 2f856f7d2721b703074c480bb6c603658873be00af0ba439408035ba3a442f24 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-f7f1936acbf50157\"></a>\n### A90 source recordings, rerecordings and a reported reproduction barrier\n\n**Source unit:** `faa-a90-original-copy-trail-2026-11-01` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-bc1a65872ab62e35d7b9).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY f7f1936acbf501579170f31d42edf9791db4f086ffa2c3b9c27a7ce335838565 -->\n# A90 source recordings, rerecordings and a reported reproduction barrier\n\nNovember 1, 2026. Existing B129 / PD01–PD04 / FAA01–FAA04 operational-record trail. This unit acquired and read a previously unacquired public memorandum and inspected adjacent held certifications/logs. It does not complete native recording authentication or the outstanding May 2003 correction and administrative-disposition investigation.\n\n**New primary record:** an October 8, 2003 Boston TRACON memorandum reports an attached certified rerecording for two positions over `1209 UTC`–`1228 UTC` on September 11, 2001. It separately reports broader Digital Audio Tape recordings held in the accident file, then unreproducible because of equipment problems. A final certification describes the limited data as derived from `DVRS` data received by the facility. These assertions must be retained together; the underlying recordings, copy-generation history, equipment records and delivery proof have not been acquired here.\n\nThis gives a specific original-versus-copy retrieval target. It does **not** establish that the DATs were destroyed, permanently unreadable, suppressed, or inconsistent with the certified copy. It also does not prove all relevant channels were delivered. Both an available certified derivative and a reported source-media access limitation appear in the same record.\n\n## Sources, acquisition and actual scope\n\n**New source — [A90-67, NARA7598345](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-A90-67-Memo-Certifying-Re-recording.pdf).** Catalog item: [official entry](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7598345). Acquired November 1, 2026 at 2026-11-01T20:15:20.135005+00:00, HTTP 200/application-pdf, **36,088 bytes**, one physical page. SHA-256: `ce72cce78d5aff847284f9df4761b62dd33e6dbb5c0b27d8385d934e3aac68e5`. The URL was taken from the rendered catalog download link, not guessed. The HTTP entity and its retrieval receipt remain preserved; this is not an original on-wire capture or native-media authentication. The catalog interface displayed `14 KB`, while the acquired entity is `36,088` bytes. These source-layer observations are retained; no earlier PDF entity/hash is available here to test versions or substitution.\n\n**Held source — [AEA1130, NARA7598367](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf).** Acquired October 30, 2026; 15,505,493 bytes, 237 physical pages, unchanged SHA-256 `5bb71c00b716fb59425571d0c714a97a32d84b13f35dcc65d7cc6bd6f83b5179`. This unit visually inspected physical **9–23**: **14 previously unread pages plus a recheck of 16**. It also read the new A90-67 one-page entity. Whole-page renders and a higher-resolution index recheck were used. No hidden PDF extraction, OCR, redaction recovery, audio acquisition/listening/decoding or physical-clock fitting occurred. Printed page numbers and dates differ by contributing facility; physical PDF page numbers below are one-based.\n\nThe [NARA finding aid](https://www.archives.gov/research/foia/faa-finding-aid) led to the new memorandum and five further item-level targets. Those five target PDFs remain **unacquired and unread**. A direct attempt to preserve the original finding-aid HTML returned HTTP 502; six relevant rendered table rows were subsequently saved through the public browser. That browser derivative is not raw HTML. The web tool also could not open the catalog item, while the public browser and linked PDF retrieval worked. These access outcomes are tool/route observations, not evidence of removal or concealment.\n\n[Source hashes and locators](CITATIONS.md#nov04-272a3fec691ac79648db) · [Selected observations](CITATIONS.md#nov04-10cc712152c5d4994dbc).\n\n## The October 8, 2003 memorandum: what it actually certifies\n\n[A90-67 physical 1](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-A90-67-Memo-Certifying-Re-recording.pdf#page=1), subject/from/to fields, three substantive passages and Attachments footer:\n\n| Passage | Preserved source report | Qualification |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| From/to and subject | Manager, Boston TRACON, A90-1 → Acting Manager, Air Traffic Division, ANE-500; ZNY-ARTCC-148 / UAL175 | Office routing and subject are visible. No transmission or recipient acknowledgment has been authenticated. |\n| First paragraph | Certified rerecording attached for Initial Departure `(ID)` and Lincoln `(SL)`, September 11, `1209 UTC`–`1228 UTC` | The one-page PDF contains the memorandum, not an acquired audio attachment. This does not establish that audio was absent from the actual dispatch or another released item. |\n| Second paragraph | Digital Audio Tape recordings from September 10, `2359Z`, through September 12, `0001Z`, contained in the accident file at Boston TRACON; equipment problems prevented reproduction at that time | This is a historical location/access assertion as of October 8, 2003, not present-day custody, media health or proof no other copy existed. |\n| Certification paragraph | Limited data derived from the `DVRS` data received by the facility, September 11, `1209 UTC`–`1228 UTC` | Preserve `DVRS` separately from the paragraph's DAT terminology. The source-to-copy relationship, generation history and technical meanings are not independently established. |\n\nArithmetic on the stated endpoints gives a **19-minute certified interval** and a **24-hour, 2-minute DAT date/time span**. These are labelled spans, not measurements of audio duration, continuous coverage, channel count or a missing percentage. The broad span and narrow certified scope cannot be subtracted into a finding that the remaining speech was withheld. No actual recording was tested.\n\n**Untested competing possibilities:** existing derivative availability despite an original-playback problem; different underlying media paths or copy-generation dates; or a broader unrecovered preservation/access failure. These are proposed explanations, not selected conclusions. The original DATs and identifiers, DVRS exports, channel/copy manifests, playback/repair/service logs and actual enclosures/receipts could distinguish them. The memorandum's express equipment explanation is material contrary evidence to assuming deliberate suppression; its explanation remains a source report requiring underlying support.\n\n## Four facility indexes are distinct records\n\n[AEA1130 physical 13–20](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=13) is the complete local Section 3 certification block, bounded by the divider at 13 and Section 4 divider at 21.\n\n| Physical page | Facility and date | Certification scope and selected audio labels |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 14 | Boston ATCT / BOS, December 21, 2001 | Listed originals on file; `Original Voice Recording(s)` and `Certified Cassette Rerecording(s)` |\n| 16 | Boston TRACON / A90, September 19, 2001 | Listed originals on file; `Original Voice Recording(s)` and `Certified Cassette Rerecording(s)` |\n| 18 | Boston ARTCC / ZBW, September 21, 2001 | Listed originals on file; includes `Original Voice Recordings` and radar data |\n| 20 | New York ARTCC / ZNY, September 11, 2001 | Certifies listed **copies** as true copies of originals on file; includes labels naming original recordings and specific data tapes |\n\nAn item called Original Voice Recordings inside a certified-copy list does not itself deliver or authenticate the original medium. Different offices, dates and certification scopes cannot be collapsed into a single current custodian or a shared media identity. All are historical assertions; signatures and today's holdings are not authenticated by this scan.\n\n**Correction to our predecessor:** the [earlier public review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/ce9df2c6e1a6dcb11bf1a31578586d00440508ee/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-position-log-crosscheck-2026-11-01/README.md) called the final A90 list item `Certified Cassette Recording(s)`. The source actually says **`Certified Cassette Rerecording(s)`**. That was our transcription/abbreviation error, not a disagreement between source versions. The earlier public commit and source bytes remain unchanged. [The explicit addendum](CITATIONS.md#nov04-9066e306c2bc8dd0c329) preserves both readings and their exact locations. This report does not silently rewrite the predecessor.\n\n## Named native-media and custody targets\n\n[Physical 20, ZNY Certified Index](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=20), middle/lower list:\n\n| Printed label | Identifier or stated range |\n| --- | --- |\n| Original SAR Tape | `NY1480-1491` |\n| Original AMPP Tape | `NY2044` |\n| Original Core Tape | `NY2116` |\n| DSR Tape | `ZNY-0226, -0241, -0230`; September 11, `0621-2359 UTC` |\n| DARC Tape | `ZNY-0400, -0319, -0402`; September 11, `0846-2359 UTC` |\n| SATORI Digital Audio Tape `(DAT)` | No individual identifier or coverage range printed in that list item |\n| Chain of Custody Documents | A record category is listed, without supplying the particular media/copy chain in this unit |\n\nThese identifiers make a more discriminating search/request target than a generic demand for all tapes. They do not prove present-day survival, actual contents, independent clocks or completeness. No acronym is expanded from memory, no SAR/AMPP/Core/SATORI/DSR/DARC medium is silently equated with the A90 DAT/DVRS material, and none is counted as an acquired native recording.\n\n## Contrary and contextual records preserved beside the indexes\n\nNormal Service Statements at physical **15 / Boston ATCT / September 20**, **17 / Boston TRACON / September 19**, and **19 / Boston ARTCC / September 25, 2001** state normal service and no pertinent transmissions. The same compilation contains operational chronologies at **9–12** describing communications, handoffs, warnings and aircraft reports. Preserve the wording beside those actual accounts. The procedural meaning and certification basis of pertinent require the applicable instructions and preparation records. Neither a global claim that no communications existed nor intentional misrepresentation follows from that word alone.\n\nThe [ZNY Daily Record, physical 22–23](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=22), provides another document representation. Selected Time/Remarks rows on 22 read:\n\n| Printed time | Reported entry, summarized |\n| --- | --- |\n| `1238` | Informed of possible AAL11 hijack |\n| `1250` | Informed of another possible hijack, UAL175 |\n| `1250` | Advised an aircraft crashed into the World Trade Center |\n| `1304` | Advised a second aircraft crashed into the World Trade Center |\n| `1305` | ZNY declared ATC zero; international traffic stopped, domestic-destination traffic diverted to Canada, and qualifying oceanic overflight continued |\n\nThe source's header says `09/11/01 00:38`. No UTC label is visible on the selected log face; the header's role as creation/entry/checking time is unverified. Do not assign those semantics or convert clocks from expectation. The log's `1238` stays beside the prior statement's visible `1240` struck-through / `1238` adjacent values; its `1305` stays beside prior accounts of distinct `1305`/`1315` actions. Agreement is not independent corroboration where author, preparation and source dependence remain unverified. It is not a first-ever institutional-knowledge measurement or proof that a direction was fully executed. Both log pages were viewed; unrelated personnel/operational details are not republished.\n\nAdditional bounded observations are retained in OBSERVATIONS.json:\n\n- Physical 11's printed chronology places `1255:52` before `1255:18`. That is an observable ordering issue; it is not a measured backwards clock or missing 34 seconds.\n- Physical 12's continued personnel table uses `RA55` for the same visible identity appearing as `D55` in the previously inspected statement/report. Preserve the labels; native role definitions and actual duty scope remain unresolved.\n- Physical 11 prints `DAL2483` in the `1254:16`, `1254:38`, `1255:18` and `1257:08` entries. This is an additional candidate for the prior [DAL2433 / DAL2437 lead](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/6e846c1ea159fde25e1291c1c5046e70e2e4de80/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-notification-personnel-originals-2026-11-01/README.md). Those earlier values remain inherited context, not a new same-flight/event join or a preferred identity selected here.\n- Physical 10 says ZNY accepted a handoff at `1237`; physical 9 says ZBW completed a handoff and transferred frequency at `1239`. Those descriptions concern different actions, so their unequal labels do not automatically establish an inconsistent event time or two-minute failure.\n\n## Deferred inputs and next feasible sources\n\nThe [finding aid](https://www.archives.gov/research/foia/faa-finding-aid) identifies **A90-71 / 7599620**, **A90-74 / 34911499**, **A90-87 / 7599635**, **A90-98 / 7599645** and **A90-99 / 34911505** as specific follow-on records. Only their selected catalog rows were read; their PDFs and any audio are unacquired. [OPEN-TODOS.md](CITATIONS.md#nov04-1d70d7cdbc981c431584) gives each exact link and discriminating use. No request or contact is authorized or has occurred.\n\nThis batch changes the next action by recovering a dated source-access statement and exact media/copy targets. It leaves the actual corrected May 2003 QFR, delivery/receipt, signed action/no-action decision and separate recommendation implementation/disposition chain unacquired. It does not substitute a 2001–2003 operational certificate for a later administrative outcome. All five work-plan tracks/eighteen substantive criteria remain open or partial; the technical evaluation and independent-audit owners retain their assignments.\n\nByte verification and labelled-interval arithmetic are documented separately from historical authentication, speech/copy comparison, independent clocks and scientific acceptance. Sources and previous records remain unchanged. Eligible research is prepared as an additive public branch; an unmerged PR will expose the explicit prior-wording qualification without overwriting history. Do not infer publication or PR creation from this prospective statement: separate receipts verify actual actions.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY f7f1936acbf501579170f31d42edf9791db4f086ffa2c3b9c27a7ce335838565 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-8714d26d40f416b1\"></a>\n### FAA notification form and personnel statements: what was recorded, and what remains unverified\n\n**Source unit:** `faa-notification-personnel-originals-2026-11-01` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-de26d84542b3424fb22f).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY 8714d26d40f416b1d5c3d0fb91fe9b145a048c73570138574c65191dfddb168c -->\n# FAA notification form and personnel statements: what was recorded, and what remains unverified\n\nNovember 1, 2026. Supplement to **B129 / PD01–PD04 / FAA01–FAA04**, not a replacement claim register or a completed correction/disposition inquiry.\n\nThe held official accident packet contains a **military-authority notification entry at 1239**, alongside personnel accounts that identify concrete source-matching targets. It also contains uncertainty, relayed information, differing call-sign readings and repeated narrative material. These records help test who reported knowing what; they do not establish the call's contents, a calibrated notification timeline, deliberate deception or an executed intercept.\n\n## Source and actual reading\n\nThis review used the already acquired **AEA1130 / NARA7598367**, a 237-page official public scan. Its original bytes remain unchanged: **15,505,493 bytes**, SHA-256 **5bb71c00b716fb59425571d0c714a97a32d84b13f35dcc65d7cc6bd6f83b5179**. Acquisition was October 30, 2026; this new reading was November 1. [Official PDF](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf); [source and page/field locators](CITATIONS.md#nov04-f330eee9cf9271ae12e2).\n\nAll physical pages **191–206** were visually read as whole-page, 1800-pixel Poppler renders. Pages191 and193 are section dividers;192 is the notification form;194–196 and198 form the first statement block;200–201 the second;202–204 the third, including repeated narrative;197 and199 are crossed-through largely uncompleted forms;205 is visually blank apart from scan artifacts. Page206 begins another statement whose continuation was **not read**. No substantive conclusion from that partial fourth statement is used here. No hidden text extraction, redaction reconstruction, new OCR, audio authentication or clock fit was performed.\n\nThe initial twelve-page scope was extended prospectively by four adjacent leaves because page202 began an account continuing beyond that boundary. This completed the surrounding context rather than treating the first leaf as the entire account. The full237-page packet and all remaining personnel statements have not been read by this unit. Prior reading and publication remain separate milestones.\n\n## 1. A recorded notification is not a content-authenticated call\n\nOn **physical192**, the military-authority row has **1239** in the time column and **Huntress ID** in the recipient field. The Washington Operations Center row also records **1239**, with **Via ROC** in its routing field. Several other rows contain **N/A**. Preserve these entries individually: N/A in one row does not erase a recorded entry in another.\n\nThe military row cites **FAA Order8020.11, paragraph66**; the form footer identifies **8020-3 (1-86)** and **Updated June13,2001**. The date field reads **9-11-01**. Those are different fields, not an authenticated time of form creation or a demonstrated policy violation. This page's time column does not itself specify a time zone. No UTC conversion, elapsed delay or first-ever military-notification time is computed here. [Form, physical192](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=192).\n\nThe surrounding UAL175 packet contains explicit **AAL11** accounts. Filing under UAL175 does not establish that the1239 entry conveyed knowledge of UAL175's hijacking, rather than AAL11 or another content scope. The entry qualifies a blanket claim that no military notification was recorded. It does not prove completed receipt, who actually heard the call, what was said, or a resulting operational action. The source recording, counterpart log, completion/receipt information and record-preparation history are the next discriminating inputs.\n\n## 2. The later Huntress exchanges remain separate records\n\nThe [previously published OMIC review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/dcdc9f647130139c60128ae3c131d40955536b34/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-omic-body-completion-2026-10-31/README.md) pins actual Huntress-labelled dialogue at physical164–169, printed17–22. Its **1326** and **1329** exchanges discuss qualified, changing reports about American11. Both the new form entry and those later transcript labels remain preserved.\n\nThey are not automatically one contradictory call or one continuous knowledge state. Different events, content, channels and record conventions may be involved; that possibility remains untested. Conversely, the form cannot be used to explain away the later disagreement. A native source-to-call join is required before calculating delay, attributing a false statement or deciding that these reports conflict about the same event. This unit read191–206; it references the earlier verified reading of164–169 rather than claiming a new independent reading or witness.\n\n## 3. Primary-target labels and visual reports have distinct origins\n\nThe R42 statement block on **194–196 and198** says another controller pointed out a primary target attributed through Boston to AAL11. It describes starting a track using **AAL11A** to follow the target. A tracking label assigned in that account is not, by itself, an independent aircraft identification.\n\nOn195, a reported visual observation associated with **USA583** suggests **FL290**; a later passage says the aircraft was not then seen, including on TCAS. On196, the writer reports another aircraft not seeing the primary target, then recounts loss of radio contact with UAL175, a changed code, and a later Delta-pilot report of United colors and **B767200**. On198, the account describes observing a descent from **28,400 to2,200 feet**, the latter being its last observed target/altitude. These are statements about display and relayed observations, not an independently decoded trajectory, authenticated aircraft identity or measured impact altitude. [194](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=194) · [195](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=195) · [196](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=196) · [198](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=198).\n\nThe distinctions matter in both directions. A failed visual search does not prove the aircraft was absent; a later identification report does not retrospectively make every earlier primary label certain. Underlying position recordings, pilot reports, radar/track data and their source/time associations are required. This does not perform or duplicate the separate recorder-decoding work.\n\n## 4. Preserve the Delta call signs without choosing one\n\nSelected handwritten occurrences on **195** read **DAL2433**; later occurrences on **196 and198** read **DAL2437**. Both readings remain recorded. No preferred call sign, cause of the difference, single-aircraft identity or second-aircraft theory is selected. Different aircraft, handwriting/transcription and recollection are possible explanations, not established results.\n\nThe native R42/radio recording, flight strips, handoff records and the original statement leaves are needed to distinguish those possibilities. These are visual readings of the released scan; no handwriting expertise or native-paper authentication is claimed. Exact locations are in SOURCE-PINS.json. The previously read OMIC altitude/source qualifications remain unchanged.\n\n## 5. Event headings, statement dates and memory limitations are not interchangeable\n\nThe R42 form on194 has a **September11,2001 /1303UTC** incident heading and a signature-date field **9/20/01**. The RA42 form on200 has the same incident date/time but a signature-date field **10/3/01**. The RA42 account on201 reserves the ability to add or change information as remembered. Both accounts state that equipment settings were not remembered. These are source qualifications, not evidence that a change actually occurred or that either source is false. [194](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=194) · [200](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=200) · [201](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=201).\n\nThe AreaB/CIC account on202 bears an event-date heading and a separate circled **5/1/02** annotation. The annotation's author and purpose have not been established; it is not silently adopted as a creation, revision or signature date. Its body reports approximately1240Z information about AAL11, a relay to the OMIC and the OMIC's claimed prior awareness. Its continuation reports a later UAL175/code-change relay and hearing a request for military aircraft. An overheard request does not establish the recipient, actual scramble, execution or calibrated time. [202](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=202) · [203–204](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=203).\n\nThe strongest contrary reading to a coordinated-deception inference is that these accounts concern different tasks and moments, contain openly stated uncertainty, and distinguish remembered reports from direct display observations. That reading does not erase their differing entries. Native versions, preparer instructions, SISO records, source audio and actual routing/receipt records could strengthen or weaken concerns.\n\n## 6. Repeated narrative and unused forms affect counting\n\nPages **203 and204** reproduce substantially the same handwritten continuation;204 includes a signature/name area absent from203's corresponding lower area. Their raster hashes differ. They remain two preserved leaves, not two independent witnesses or two verified revisions. The crossed-through forms197/199 and blank205 do not by themselves establish that a statement was removed, destroyed or suppressed. Their reason for inclusion is unknown. [203](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=203) · [204](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=204).\n\n## Remaining work and publication boundaries\n\nThis adds source-level notification and witness-account roots. It does **not** recover the corrected May2003 answer and delivery receipt, signed administrative action/no-action determination, recommendation implementation/closure, native recorder/clock or materials inputs, or steel attribution decisions. All five tracks and eighteen substantive requirements remain open/partial. [TODOs](CITATIONS.md#nov04-1b30096375aa39c8f594) retain those dependencies.\n\nRaw PDF/raster/signature/contact data and private coordination remain outside this public payload. No employee names, personal/contact fields or obscured identities are reproduced. The original source, preceding reports and every conflicting value remain unchanged. [Checklist](CITATIONS.md#nov04-060e789de10b5e43869a), [additive diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-a904a5a43e50a3913dea), [verifier](CITATIONS.md#nov04-642ae9294e77de1c15e1) and [actual verification scope](CITATIONS.md#nov04-741a99a6e5f430ca676d) separate preservation checks from historical conclusions. Publication is neither main-branch adoption nor substantive goal acceptance.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY 8714d26d40f416b1d5c3d0fb91fe9b145a048c73570138574c65191dfddb168c -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-f913b44363a21a3b\"></a>\n### Actual FAA Operations Manager dialogue: twelve new page readings\n\n**Source unit:** `faa-omic-body-2026-10-31` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-7fc963ab2269c781ca35).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY f913b44363a21a3b0328f92b13f146a7eec7c6c7c012d068d2ffce3f136f7e77 -->\n# Actual FAA Operations Manager dialogue: twelve new page readings\n\nOctober 31, 2026. **The actual dialogue supplies more precise notification, uncertainty and military-response leads than the previously read cover and endpoint.** It records airline and FAA information exchanges, qualified aircraft identification, an unresolved visual-verification account, an unidentified unanswered-call complaint and a report of military aircraft airborne. These are attributed statements in a released transcript, not independently verified events or proof of misconduct.\n\n[Official NARA PDF](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=149), SHA-256 `5bb71c00b716fb59425571d0c714a97a32d84b13f35dcc65d7cc6bd6f83b5179`. The previously acquired entity contains 237 physical pages and 15,505,493 bytes. Its original acquisition is documented in the [packet study](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/e64f8f3006879bfde9fa76f1db4b9be6b514d5af/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-completed-package-original-2026-10-30/README.md); no redownload occurred.\n\nWe newly read physical pages **149–160**, actually printed **2–13 of 26**, in twelve whole-page renders. The cover at 148 and terminal page at 173 were previously read. **Physical 161–172 remains unread.** This is therefore a partial Operations Manager transcript review, not a complete 26-page block or whole-packet examination. Across the checked study lineage, 32 distinct packet images have been read and 205 remain unread. Those counts describe scope, not evidentiary strength.\n\n[OBSERVATIONS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-0fd213897e5edb3dd123) separates printed statements from inference. [SOURCE-PINS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-34b45bd7165e9debb21e) supplies exact pages, row times, source and raster hashes. [OPEN-TODOS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-ec49d7969bdd80da4ecc) preserves the records needed to test each account. Original source bytes, conflicting labels and prior reports remain unchanged.\n\n## What the body actually says\n\n| Pin and source locator | Newly read observation | Implication and remaining limit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| OM01: physical 149–153, printed 2–6; 1241:14, 1244:23, 1245:35 | UAL175 heading/certification coexists with actual American 11 dialogue. Page 149 begins with bare 1230–1240 markers before timed row 1240:14. | A case heading does not identify every conversation's aircraft. Bare markers do not prove acoustic silence or missing speech. |\n| OM02: 151/153/155; 1244:27/30, 1245:44 and 1246:49–54 | Earlier verification and “two nine zero” wording coexist with later “at thirty one,” “did not verify,” “at twenty nine” and “did not see him” wording. | Preserve both reports and their source expressions. Do not silently convert “thirty one” into a formal flight level. Actual altitude, identity and cause remain unverified. |\n| OM03: 152–153; 1245:32–1246:04 | Boston/New York role-coded dialogue attributes information to the airline and reports cockpit microphone broadcasts and threats. | This identifies original radio/telephone counterpart targets. The relay is not a CVR or independent validation of the underlying broadcast or threat. |\n| OM04: 154–155; 1246:40–54 | Requests to keep other traffic away accompany the later no-visual-confirmation report. | Requested protective work is contrary to a universal no-action account, but does not establish actual execution of every instruction or a verified military response. |\n| OM05: 155–157; 1249:01–25 and 1252:55 | An AEA500-coded inquiry receives a hijack/position report; the ATM role later reports using two phones. | These are concrete role/time/source pointers. Labels do not authenticate voices, call-leg topology, bridge participation or the cause of a later unanswered call. |\n| OM06: 158–159; 1252:59–1253:43 | Fire/possible-impact and looked-like-explosion descriptions are relayed with Newark/media attribution. | Preserve appearances and their source chain. This is not independently heard explosive evidence, a chemical finding or a demolition-mechanism identification; it supplies no WTC 7 connection. |\n| OM07: 159; 1300:56, 1301:40, 1302:06 | Unanswered-phone complaints coexist with reported military aircraft airborne, F15s and a target point-out concerning a United aircraft. | The unanswered recipient and exact call leg are unidentified in this passage. Do not substitute NORAD/Huntress from a legend, or treat an airborne report as verified interception. |\n| OM08: 159–160; 1303:17, 1310:24–29, 1311:09 | Ground-stop/ATC-zero discussion, a Cleveland-role query/answer and a Command Center NOM update request occur in the body. | These supply counterpart/order/log targets. A caller's negative-framed question is not an established opposite status; New York's reply reports ATC zero. No delivery delay or independent clock offset is measured. |\n| OM09: 160; 1311:20–33 | New York's role disclaims confirmed aircraft identification, then reports suspicion of American 11 and United 175. | Preserve the qualification. A later packet heading is not contemporaneous certainty or independent proof of occupants or cause. |\n\nAll times are **printed labels**. The previously read cover declares 1230–1344 UTC and bears February 4, 2002. That declaration does not calibrate a source clock, authenticate a native version or signature, or show what a later reviewer received. No conversion into measured impact times or elapsed delays was performed.\n\n## Conflicts and uncertainty remain explicit\n\n**Verification and identity:** earlier verification/altitude expressions and the later no-visual-confirmation account remain side by side. Different observers, checks, targets or times, relay error and unsupported assurance are untested possibilities. Original radar/aircraft reports and matched audio would distinguish them. The later explicit uncertainty about identifying the two aircraft is contrary evidence to treating every earlier remark as confirmed knowledge.\n\n**Telephone failure and military activity:** unanswered calls and aircraft reportedly airborne are different claims, not an automatic contradiction. The recipient is unresolved; one role's report is not independent confirmation of an intercept or intentional refusal. The two-phone remark is retained as a separate source statement, not selected as the cause of the later complaint. Obtain source-specific dialing, recipient and counterpart records before attributing the failed contact.\n\n**Incident labels and appearances:** American 11 dialogue in a UAL175-headed transcript demonstrates that headings and individual conversation subjects need not match exclusively. This qualifies an exclusive-flight interpretation; it does **not** resolve the separate AWA937 AAL11/ZBW versus UAL175 work-file inconsistency in the [five-item comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/e4e27ee8cd54f00d62917b2b9181d22503d2df9e/investigation/broader-evidence/five-inconsistency-tests-2026-10-31/README.md). Relayed impact, fire and explosion appearances likewise are not competing forensic mechanisms by themselves. No source is selected as controlling.\n\n## Next records and the original investigation\n\nThese actual body passages provide more precise acquisition targets than a legend alone: matching Boston, regional-office, Command Center and other-center recordings; call-leg and failed-attempt records; actual ground-stop orders; radar/other-aircraft reports; and their clock/custody chains. A generic NOM recording label does not identify a particular call or channel.\n\nNo Huntress-coded timed row was observed in the twelve pages inspected. This is **not** an absence claim for unread 161–172, other channels, the complete line or historical contacts. The remaining held body is available work under the next prospective reading scope.\n\nThe actual May 2003 draft/answer/corrected version/receipt, signed administrative consideration/action/no-action, and separate recommendation response/implementation/closure remain missing inputs. This transcript does not establish which later official read which source version or knowingly made an inaccurate statement. Test a concealment interpretation by matching material information to actual received/reviewed records and the exact later statement or decision. Different source scopes, uncertain identifications, accurate qualifications, documented corrections or substantive follow-through could weaken that interpretation. None is adopted to explain away the unresolved record.\n\nAll 18 original FAA, telephone, recorder, materials and steel acceptance criteria remain open or partial. Actual source reading is progress; publication and hashes do not close required original, custody or empirical joins. Independent decoding and calibrated clock tests remain assigned to the technical owner. Available unread body pages prevent a premature whole-goal blocked determination.\n\n## Preservation, checks and revision accountability\n\nThe held input fingerprint was checked before rendering. Twelve new rasters and their hashes remain in the verified source store. [SOURCE-CHECKS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-c906bd8d114e8dd8ef4f) records the actual scope. Reading is separate from rendering and hashing. No new download, OCR, hidden-layer extraction, masked-character recovery, listening, waveform/decoder work, independent clock/radar fit, exhaustive text comparison or scientific/historical authentication occurred. Faint or occluded characters remain unknown.\n\nBefore publication, preliminary shorthand expanded the later spoken “thirty one” into 310. That was our interpretation, not a verbatim source field. The preliminary local record and its correction remain preserved; this edition retains the source expressions. No source bytes or conflicting evidence were changed. [EDITORIAL-REVISION.diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-aeb9f1c10b7f559a287c) preserves this edition's immediate-predecessor text changes; [TEXT-CHANGES.diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-79682352af7ae8d577db) records the actual additions against the new public directory's preceding absent state. PDF/image reading is separate from both text diffs. Earlier failed OCR checks and reading corrections remain unchanged.\n\n[CHECKLIST.md](CITATIONS.md#nov04-a758f817984bd1bdca62) accounts for the requirements. Only eligible original notes, selected safe fields, source pins, qualifications and TODOs form the public derivative. Excluded: raw marked/protected sources, full speech, signatures, identifying/contact/access/financial data, lawsuit material, private correspondence/strategy and credentials. The separate actual AveLyra noreply/account/destination/SSH/ref/all-file receipt establishes publication, not historical truth. No outreach, fee, request, filing, mailbox/private-case access, source overwrite, deletion, private push, main merge or history rewrite occurred.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY f913b44363a21a3b0328f92b13f146a7eec7c6c7c012d068d2ffce3f136f7e77 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-cd092d9bfcf68aa2\"></a>\n### FAA Operations Manager transcript: remaining body and notification-source leads\n\n**Source unit:** `faa-omic-body-completion-2026-10-31` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-982c474f733b0363f0f5).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY cd092d9bfcf68aa2d2c39f4730edceff3e1599cfeaf079e6e82598c7c23083d7 -->\n# FAA Operations Manager transcript: remaining body and notification-source leads\n\nReviewed October 31, 2026. This is a new source-reading supplement to the [first-half review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/9c1438f5fb3b9098513db5bad9c5a13f6bb127ea/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-omic-body-2026-10-31/README.md), not a replacement for it. No source bytes or earlier findings were edited.\n\nThe useful new lead is a traceable information chain about **American 11 allegedly remaining airborne**. One Huntress-labelled exchange cannot confirm it; a later exchange reports it affirmatively and invokes a mission crew commander. The transcript then records New York relaying a separate criticism of Washington Center and Washington questioning that criticism. These are statements in an officially released scan, not independent authentication of the underlying events or proof of deliberate deception.\n\n## Source and actual scope\n\n[NARA catalogue, NAID7598367](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7598367) · [official FAA AEA1130 PDF](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf)\n\nSource SHA-256: `5bb71c00b716fb59425571d0c714a97a32d84b13f35dcc65d7cc6bd6f83b5179`. The held 237-page, 15,505,493-byte PDF was acquired October 30, 2026, and rechecked unchanged for this unit. Newly read: **physical pages161–172, printed14–25 of26**, individually viewed as whole-page rasters. The preceding studies read the cover148, body149–160 and end173. Thus this specific 26-page block has now been visually read across those studies. That does not authenticate its source recording, completeness, voices, clock, custody or all other records. Across the checked packet lineage,44distinct pages have been read and193remain outside that scope. Counts measure reading coverage, not proof.\n\n`SOURCES.json` gives each page's first/last timed row. `SOURCE-PINS.json` supplies physical/printed page, row time, source URL/hash, selected literal wording and qualifications for OM10–OM17. Times below retain the printed clock (HHMM:SS); UTC is declared on the transcript cover, not independently calibrated here. No seconds-level cross-record alignment was performed.\n\n## Conflicting or changing reports, preserved side by side\n\n| Source locator | What the source reports | Limit / discriminating record |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| OM13, physical164–166 / printed17–19,1326:18–1327:24 | Actual Huntress-labelled rows. New York says it was given a number and, at1326:47, describes **an unconfirmed report** that American11 might remain airborne. At1327:21 Huntress is unsure and cannot confirm either way. | Native audio/call-leg identity and the report's source are unverified. Earlier unanswered-phone passages remain unidentified; this later contact does not identify their recipient. |\n| OM14, physical166–168 / printed19–21,1329:28–1330:23 | A later Huntress-labelled opening says the caller was told to call;1329:32 invokes the mission crew commander. Qualifiers accompany the three-aircraft/two-impacts/one-toward-Washington account. At1329:50/53 the caller says American11 was **not the crashed aircraft** and was **still airborne**. At1329:59 current location is unknown and Washington is last-known direction. New York says it had not received this information. | Preserve alongside OM13 and earlier suspected impact identification. Do not assume the same caller, source, correctly identified aircraft or validated route. Retrieve the mission-crew-command input/instruction, plots, counterpart recordings and correction trail. |\n| OM15, physical169 / printed22,1330:39–56 | Huntress criticizes Washington, clarifies **Washington Center**, and assents to New York's paraphrase about not taking it seriously. New York promises to let Washington know. | An attributed criticism and a promise, not established Washington conduct or a completed relay. |\n| OM16, physical170–171 / printed23–24,1331:46–1332:31 | New York relays the Huntress contact. At1331:52 a ZDC OMIC-labelled row says **“we got the same call”** and mentions an air major/Boston contact. At1332:08 New York qualifies its account as **“this is only relay”**. After New York relays the seriousness criticism, a Washington speaker says they do not know what is meant and, at1332:29, **“i haven't even i haven't even talked to em”**, then promises a call. | Both statements retained. The shared ZDC role label does not prove one individual; holds/introductions intervene. Receiving an American11 report also need not mean receiving the seriousness criticism. The difference is unresolved, not a proved single-person contradiction. |\n| OM17, physical172 / printed25,1333:35–1334:00 | ZNY505 reports American77 lost at **flight level three five zero** near the printed **“york kentucky”** wording and requests checks for primary targets. At1333:52 **“went down”** is immediately qualified by **“didn't go down”** and **“they just lost everything”**. | Preserve the wording; do not silently repair geography or convert loss of contact into a crash. The earlier generic Indianapolis/American report on164 is not independently joined to this aircraft. |\n\nThe strongest objection to treating this as intentional false information is that these are changing, sometimes explicitly unconfirmed relays during an unfolding event, with incomplete call-leg/speaker/source information. That possibility is not established either. The native messages, validation/correction steps, actual recipients and counterpart audio could distinguish ordinary error or relay confusion from knowingly false reporting. No originator, intent, causal scramble or cover-up is established by this reading.\n\n## Other concrete leads and contrary evidence\n\n- **Military detail remains qualified (OM10).** At1311:53 and1312:01, physical161 / printed14, ZNY reports military aircraft up but does not know count/location/origin. Keep this beside earlier F15/point-out reports on159, rather than assume inconsistency or verified interception.\n- **Headquarters telcon: request and plan, not attendance (OM11).** Physical162–164 / printed15–17: AEA500 asks a manager to join at1318:07; an Unknown-labelled speaker refers to getting a number at1320:15; ATM says going to the telcon at1325:04. Actual participation, bridge topology and any military participation remain unverified.\n- **Area B staff hold/discussions (OM12).** Physical163–164 / printed16–17,1320:20 and1325:06/17: a hold-before-discussion instruction coexists with breaks/discussions and shaken-staff descriptions. This warrants retrieving the actual instructions, independent statements and tape/custody crosswalk. It does not establish coaching, fabricated accounts or destruction, and is not automatically the separately described recorded-statements tape.\n- **Readiness wording (OM15).** Physical168 / printed21,1330:09–20 contains readiness discussion and a claim of a recent increase. It gives no authenticated formal order, level or effective time. A comment about not saying too much is not proof of concealment.\n\n## What changed, what remains open\n\nThe prior review's absence of a Huntress-coded row was expressly limited to149–160. The new rows are on164–169. The earlier bounded observation remains accurate; it must not be generalized to the whole block or earliest historical contact. These studies are dependent readings of one released scan, not independent corroborating records.\n\nOnly **OM-T08 (remaining page reading)** is completed. OM-T01–07 remain TODO_INPUT; OM-T09–12 identify the new source-input, receiving/callback, staff-discussion and order/radar dependencies. The five-track plan's original18requirements all remain open/partial. This transcript does not establish which later officials read which version, the actual May2003 corrected answer/delivery, a signed2006 action/no-action determination or recommendation disposition.\n\nMissing inputs stay TODOs. No outreach, request, fees, mailbox access, technical decoding or experiment occurred. Original sources and conflicting values remain unchanged. Source integrity checks in `SOURCE-CHECKS.json` are separate from visual reading and do not authenticate historical truth. `CHECKLIST.md` maps the user's requirements; `TEXT-CHANGES.diff` is the actual Git addition diff against absent prior paths. Raw marked source pages, full speech, personal/contact/access/signature fields and private case material are excluded from this public derivative. Publication status is established separately by the remote receipt, not this prepublication text.\n\nPrepublication editorial review corrected the agent draft's physical161 terminal row from1312:15 to1312:13 and replaced paraphrased/typographically changed fragments in literal-quotation fields with the inspected source wording. These were errors in our draft, not discrepancies in the released evidence. The source and v1 draft remain preserved; `EDITORIAL-REVISION.diff` records the exact changes.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY cd092d9bfcf68aa2d2c39f4730edceff3e1599cfeaf079e6e82598c7c23083d7 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-f9aab6baa4dcec3a\"></a>\n### FAA personnel accounts: military contacts, routing and correction instructions\n\n**Source unit:** `faa-personnel-continuation-2026-11-01` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-8db4795a042922bee8ae).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY f9aab6baa4dcec3a2eafb812928c4e9fcab873f2f9eb3f7cbc050d678a1eda4c -->\n# FAA personnel accounts: military contacts, routing and correction instructions\n\nNovember 1, 2026. Existing **B129 / PD01–PD04 / FAA01–FAA04**. This additive supplement completes reading the personnel section reproduced in the held packet. It does not complete the missing FAA correction/disposition investigation.\n\nThe most useful new record is the military-operations specialist's two-page account of obtaining a NEADS number and later requesting jets. It supplies a closer comparison for the earlier **1239** notification entry. The records do not yet establish that those references describe the same call, flight, content or time convention. The underlying recordings and routing records are the discriminating evidence.\n\n## Source and completed reading scope\n\n**AEA1130/NARA7598367** was acquired October30,2026 from the [official download](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf). Its unchanged entity is **15,505,493 bytes**, SHA-256 **5bb71c00b716fb59425571d0c714a97a32d84b13f35dcc65d7cc6bd6f83b5179**. This unit visually read physical **207–234** at1800pixels, and rechecked208/210/211 with fresh3000pixel renders. The detail renders are another display of the same file, not an independent record or handwriting authentication. No OCR, hidden text extraction or redaction reconstruction was performed.\n\nTogether with the [preceding191–206 review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/6e846c1ea159fde25e1291c1c5046e70e2e4de80/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-notification-personnel-originals-2026-11-01/README.md), this covers the personnel section between divider193 and the weather-section divider232. Page206 is prior context, not counted again as a new page. This unit read the OSIC continuation207, OMIC208–209, military specialist210–211, air traffic manager212–214/216/218, assistant manager220–221, traffic-management accounts222–225, R55226/228 and D55230–231. Blank or largely unused leaves215/217/219/227/229 remain preserved.233–234 were viewed as adjacent weather context; no new meteorological inference or full-weather-section claim is made. This is not a complete reading or authentication of all237pages, all agency statements or every retained version.\n\nPhysical page and named field/paragraph references below are precise scan locators, not invented original line numbers. [SOURCE-PINS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-26e25051b641ed4d16c4) includes acquisition dates, source/raster hashes, reading scope and the selected observations. Raw scans, signatures, employee names, full telephone numbers and the printed telcon access details remain outside this public payload.\n\n## 1. Preserve the notification entry and the specialist's account separately\n\n| Record | Displayed entry or reported event | What it does not establish |\n|---|---|---|\n| Previously read physical192, military-authority row | **1239 /Huntress ID**; the caller-field name corresponds to the writer identified on210 |Native identity/call join, contents, time zone, receipt or flight-specific knowledge.|\n| Physical210, opening body |At **1249**, a request to obtain a NEADS telephone number; the writer says a VSCS call obtained a number, which was passed on |That this is the same event as the1239 notification row, an exact clock reading, or an operational scramble request.|\n| Physical210–211, continuation |**Maybe10minutes later, maybe less**, a manager requested a call asking for jets because another hijack was possible |An exact second-call time, authenticated request receipt or actual launch.|\n| Physical211, phone description |A separate phone beside the VSCS is followed by the circled parenthetical **1429** |That1429 is a time rather than an extension/position/other identifier. Its meaning is unestablished.|\n| Physical211, end of account |The writer reports giving a call sign, possible transponder code and brief summary; NEADS reportedly said Boston had also requested jets |Independent confirmation of the words, Boston request, knowledge of every official or operational execution.|\n| Physical216,1304Z-labelled paragraph |A manager says the specialist reported the military had scrambled and wanted the current aircraft location |An independently verified scramble order, launch time, aircraft assignment or interception.|\n\n[210](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=210) · [211](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=211) · [216](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=216). The specialist form's signature-date field reads **09/19/01**; it is a released recollection, not a live source recording or authenticated native signed original. The word-level readings1249 and1429 were checked against the3000pixel displays. No elapsed delay or synthesized historical chronology is computed.\n\nThe1239/1249 records are an **unresolved comparison requiring an event join**, not proof of a ten-minute delay or false entry. Obtaining a number, notifying, requesting jets, reporting a scramble and actual execution are distinct acts. Different calls, recollection/entry errors, differing time conventions and other explanations remain testable possibilities. None is selected as the cause. The later1326/1329 Huntress transcript exchanges already published remain another set of records; their disagreement is not resolved by either account.\n\n## 2. Telcon instructions are concrete routing leads, not attendance proof\n\nThe OMIC account on208 says the writer was asked to join a telcon with **CF2/BOS and military** about AAL11. Its opening time contains **1240** struck through with **1238** beside it. Both visible values remain; no preferred historical time or amendment chronology is adopted. The body also reports monitoring, dispatcher information and other relays; the signature-date field is **9/14/01**. [208](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=208).\n\nThe air traffic manager's account on218 says a regional official directed immediate participation in a **national telcon**. That direction alone does not prove the writer joined, who else was present, that this was the telcon described on208, or who received a particular statement. The full telephone/access details are excluded here. [218](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=218).\n\nThe212–218 account also describes attempts to reach regional personnel, holds and unavailable recipients, a request to check television reports, and later briefings. These are participants' reports to match to original lines, bridge records, attendance and counterpart logs. Different attempted/completed call legs cannot be collapsed into a single agency-wide knowledge state. Nor do the written accounts alone establish coordinated delay or concealment.\n\n## 3. Stop accepting traffic, clear sectors and declare ATC zero are different outcomes\n\nThe previously incomplete OSIC account206–207 is now read through its end. The opening reports stopping incoming AreaB traffic before a named route point; the continuation describes tracking/avoiding UAL175, seeing its target disappear, receiving impact news, relieving upset controllers and consolidating sectors after their aircraft cleared. It states equipment settings were not remembered. This is not evidence of coaching or suppression of accounts. [207](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=207).\n\nThe OMIC account on209 records a **1315** statement that facilities could go ATC zero while retaining airspace, and a **1325** oceanic-diversion report. The manager account on216 separately records a **1305Z** direction to declare New York Center ATC zero. The traffic-management account on225 says ATCSCC was notified at approximately **1305Z** that ZNY was ATC zero and entering traffic should stop. The earlier RA42 account on201 describes deciding to close that sector after the target disappeared. [209](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=209) · [216](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=216) · [225](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=225).\n\nPreserve **1305/1315** and their different scope/wording. Do not infer one ten-minute system delay, a stand-down order or a preferred time. Local traffic restrictions, a sector closure, facility declaration, relayed authorization and retained airspace need their own source/receipt/execution joins. The recorded actions are counterevidence to a blanket claim that nobody attempted protective action; they do not establish that every action was timely or effective.\n\n## 4. Local awareness and failed counterpart recognition remain separate observations\n\nThe traffic-management account224–225 reports that AreaB notified OMIC/TMU of a deviation around **1250Z**, followed by display observations of **code3321**. It recounts N90 initially not identifying or displaying that target around **1300Z**, then reporting an unidentified9500foot target around **1302Z**, and an apparent crash around **1303Z**. These are remembered, approximately timed relays, not verified original N90 utterances or independently calibrated coverage. [224](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=224) · [225](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=225).\n\nThe R55/D55 accounts226/228/230/231 separately describe tracking/point-outs, traffic-avoidance actions and an intruder code3321. The D55 continuation describes asking Approach to hold not-yet-departed traffic and reports its last observed hit at **2200feet**. Preserve their qualifications and settings-memory limitations. This is not measured impact altitude or independent aircraft identity. [228](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=228) · [231](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=231).\n\nThese accounts supply specific facilities, roles and candidate source events. Differences in identification/display or relayed certainty could reflect different coverage, times, tasks or records; deliberate misstatement remains an unproved hypothesis. Source recordings, display settings, radar/track data, flight strips and counterpart records are required to discriminate. This unit performs no new recorder decoding or clock alignment and does not duplicate the technical owner's work.\n\n## 5. Compare visible amendments with the held correction instructions\n\nThe previously published **AEA1121** correction list, physical9/printed8of8, directs handwritten equipment-configuration statements and conditional removal/initialling of the Attachment label. It separately asks for an OSIC accident-time change to **1303**, asks whether the assistant-manager heading is **1303 or1309**, and requests a D55 location of **New York,NY**. Its marked responses say changes were made. These are source instructions/judgments, not our adoption of their correctness. [Original list](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1121-MEMORANDUM--FORMAL-ACCIDENT-PACKAGE-REF-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-CORRECTIONS.pdf#page=9) · [earlier full review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/a42d0956202dacc3b971d21f479966d56fefcd60/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-package-version-chain-2026-10-30/README.md).\n\nThe now-read forms contain corresponding features: handwritten settings-memory statements and struck preprinted/Attachment labels; the206 header has1303 with an earlier time struck;220 has1303 above a struck time field;230 has a New York location alongside struck prior location text. The body's other events are not automatically retimed by an incident-header change. Selected visible call-sign headers on208/210/212/226/230 likewise contain crossed AAL11 and written UAL175 labels, while bodies still discuss AAL11. Both labels and the bodies remain preserved.\n\nThis correspondence is an observable version-control lead, **not native authentication that the memo caused every mark**, that all requested edits were properly implemented, or that any change occurred after signing. The memo supplies an explicit ordinary review context for some annotations; it does not explain every call-sign/body/time difference. Original and amended leaves, approvals/initials, submission/receipt and actual change history remain necessary. No record is overwritten or adjudicated here. These2002 package corrections are not the missing May2003 QFR correction or2006 administrative outcome.\n\n## Completion and remaining dependencies\n\nThe bounded personnel-section reading dependency is complete **for this reproduced packet**, with blank/repeated/annotated leaves preserved. It is not a claim that every statement is complete, independently corroborative, historically accurate or natively authenticated. One declared indexed search for a1429/ZNY archive locator returned no result; no recording was acquired from that search, and no nonexistence conclusion follows.\n\nAll five tracks and eighteen substantive requirements remain open/partial. Actual May2003 drafts/correction/delivery, signed administrative action/no-action/reasons and distinct recommendation outcomes are still missing. Telephone native joins, recorder/custody/clocks, materials specimen/calibration records and steel preservation/attribution remain required. [OPEN-TODOS.md](CITATIONS.md#nov04-219e86ef09f26ba475e4) carries them forward. [Checklist](CITATIONS.md#nov04-84949969d036a6dd8225), [revision diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-fedc78f2ae4224356202), [verifier](CITATIONS.md#nov04-8a56c2e94dcf74e7dd69) and [verification scope](CITATIONS.md#nov04-8e8fc13f368ef552fcc2) distinguish byte/reading checks from historical inference, publication and whole-goal acceptance.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY f9aab6baa4dcec3a2eafb812928c4e9fcab873f2f9eb3f7cbc050d678a1eda4c -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-76abaaa5ebacde0c\"></a>\n### FAA position records: four time matches, distinct labels and unresolved clocks\n\n**Source unit:** `faa-position-log-crosscheck-2026-11-01` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-61a04418560c96ca4eeb).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY 76abaaa5ebacde0c0d5cdd30da5cb4c815b11c216f2d4eac901276e7b9817920 -->\n# FAA position records: four time matches, distinct labels and unresolved clocks\n\nResearch date: November 1, 2026. Existing B129 / PD01–PD04 / FAA01–FAA04 record trail. This is one bounded inspection of held public primary records, not a new download, a whole-packet authentication or completion of the original-record work plan.\n\nThe located position report supplies all four preselected on/off intervals, and each agrees with the corresponding statement's time fields after removing the report's colon punctuation. This is useful for locating the underlying records. **It is not independent validation:** the statement forms direct writers to make their times correspond with SISO. One position label remains different, `RA42` versus `D42`; the report's timezone and historical clock accuracy are not established. Separate roster and overtime records also prevent treating an `RDO` entry as standalone evidence that a writer was absent.\n\nA separate within-form discrepancy is preserved: two weather descriptions and their adjacent time boxes differ by four hours while both label their values UTC. The values' cause and effect remain untested. These findings do not establish coordinated falsification, foreknowledge, suppression or a cover-up.\n\n## Source and reading scope\n\nOfficial held source: [AEA1130 / NARA7598367, completed accident package](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf), acquired October 30, 2026, 18:22:50 UTC; 15,505,493 bytes, 237 physical PDF pages. SHA-256: `5bb71c00b716fb59425571d0c714a97a32d84b13f35dcc65d7cc6bd6f83b5179`. Physical PDF page numbers below are one-based; printed sheet pagination differs. The held bytes were rehashed and preserved unchanged. The public derivative contains research text, selected field transcriptions and provenance, without the raw scan, personnel roster, names, signatures or contact/access details.\n\nActually inspected in this unit: **27 distinct new whole pages — 5, 8, 16 and 24–47**. Page 39 received an additional whole-page 3000-pixel legibility render; that is not an additional source page. Previously inspected statement faces 194, 200, 226 and 230 were rechecked as context, not counted as new acquisition or new pages. On the position printouts the relevant columns/selected rows and boundaries were read; faint, cropped and unrelated totals were not exhaustively transcribed. The other 237-page packet contents have not all been reviewed by this unit.\n\nThe frozen four-row selection preceded complete-log reading. Four directly relevant positions were retained regardless of outcome; no failed or inconvenient row was replaced. Visible name/operating-initial cross-references support the candidate row joins, but native identity and custody have not been authenticated. Source faces, render hashes, precise column locators and all selected strings are in [SOURCE-PINS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-1a5d52011dda862b424c) and [COMPARISON.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-5efab08d95fdca1a9443). No hidden text extraction, new OCR, redaction reconstruction or source-image editing occurred.\n\n## What the physical sequence contains\n\n[Physical 24–36](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=24) includes the Section 5 Personnel Logs divider; Area B personnel, additional-personnel, OSIC/CIC certification, correction and assignment-change forms; and Traffic Management forms. The sign-in/sign-out correction form on physical 32 / printed 7 of 8 has an unused body. That does not prove no corrections occurred elsewhere. Physical 31 has populated certification time fields; physical 33 has assignment changes. Those records have distinct purposes and must not be collapsed into the blank correction form.\n\nThe **Section 6 Automated Sign-In/Sign-Out (SISO) Data divider is physical 37**, immediately followed by the **Section 7 Facility Layout Chart divider on physical 38**. A position report containing the selected rows is at **physical 39**, with further printout material at 40–42; facility drawings follow at 43–44. Physical 45 is the Section 8 divider; physical 46 contains two UAL175 flight strips; physical 47 begins Section 9 Transcriptions of Voice Recordings. The misplaced-looking divider sequence is preserved as observed. It does not show why the sequence exists, that a page was deliberately removed, or that SISO data are globally absent. Reading the divider alone would have missed the available report.\n\nPhysical 40 carries a representation notice: the document derives from magnetic recordings of internal computer processing and is not an exact representation of the control-position display. That is a scope qualification, not a finding of tampering, a guarantee of raw-data completeness, or proof of what a controller saw. Physical 44 labels an Area B layout position `D42`, separately from `R42`; it does not by itself authenticate the statement's `RA42` terminology, actual staffing or September 11 hardware/display state.\n\n## Four selected position intervals\n\nStatement position fields and on/off fields are on [194](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=194), [200](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=200), [226](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=226) and [230](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=230). The report's POSITION, TIME-ON and TIME-OFF columns are on [39](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=39). Selected row locators below avoid reproducing personnel identities.\n\n| Statement face and literal position label | Statement fields, UTC printed beside fields | Report position and selected row | Report TIME-ON / TIME-OFF | Bounded result |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 194, `RADAR Sector42` | `1207`–`1319` | `R42`, second R42 row | `12:07`–`13:19` | Both time strings match after colon removal. `R42` is the research role shorthand; literal labels remain distinct. |\n| 200, `RA42` | `1255`–`1319` | `D42`, row immediately below the two R42 rows | `12:55`–`13:19` | Time strings match; `RA42` / `D42` label difference remains preserved. |\n| 226, `R55` | `1120`–`1316` | `R55`, sixth R55 row | `11:20`–`13:16` | Both time strings match. |\n| 230, `D55` | `1132`–`1316` | `D55`, third D55 row | `11:32`–`13:16` | Both time strings match. |\n\n**What was tested:** punctuation-only comparison of eight selected time fields, with source strings retained. All four on/off pairs agree. This is a transcribed-field consistency test, not a measurement of clock error. No timezone label is visible on the inspected position-report face; its case context and surrounding dated records are not a substitute for an authenticated export/date/timezone definition.\n\n**Strongest limitation on corroboration:** all four statement faces explicitly require statement times and dates to correspond with SISO. Their agreement is compatible with writers consulting the same administrative source. The actual preparation history remains unknown; we have not proved copying in any individual instance. The agreement cannot be multiplied into four independent confirmations of clock accuracy, identity, continuous presence, what was heard, or every statement narrative.\n\nThe prior [personnel continuation review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/59828ff9efa5ee2bf073ebf3796a012b35bdd180/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-personnel-continuation-2026-11-01/README.md) retains the January 2002 correction memorandum's account that RA42 and D42 refer to the same sector. That is the memorandum author's characterization, not this investigation's adjudication of equivalent positions or duties. The newly located report preserves `D42` alongside `RA42`; no label is substituted or made controlling. Underlying position definitions and amendments remain TODOs.\n\n## Shift records differ in scope from position records\n\nThe Personnel Log's Time on / Time off fields cover shift/attendance-style entries; the position report and statements cover particular positions. No timezone is visible on the selected roster sheets. The research has not converted a shift time into UTC or treated unlike intervals as a contradiction.\n\n| Role used to locate the same statement author's row | Personnel Log fields | Additional context | Consequence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| R42 | Physical 26 / printed 1 of 8: `0630`–`1430` | Position interval above is `1207`–`1319` UTC | Different scope and unestablished clock basis; no attendance residual calculated. |\n| RA42 | Physical 28 / printed 3 of 8: `0630`–`1430` | Released POS area obscured | Do not reconstruct the hidden POS field or infer its exact label. |\n| R55 | Physical 28 / printed 3 of 8: `0657`–`1457` | Statement interval `1120`–`1316` UTC | Both records retained without timezone conversion. |\n| D55 | Physical 26: Time on `RDO`, Time off blank | Physical 30, Operational Overtime or Additional Personnel, has same-name/initial entries `0645`–`1445` with `8.0` duty hours, and `0615`–`0645` with `.5` | Preserve all entries; `RDO` alone does not establish absence or false duty. |\n\n`RDO` is left unexpanded because the native legend and roster definitions have not been obtained. The additional-personnel rows and position interval are material contrary evidence to an absence inference. They also do not, without authentication and clock definitions, prove physical attendance or resolve the records' administrative meaning. Overtime labels, signatures or correction marks are not motive evidence.\n\n## Weather fields: two explicit UTC-labelled mismatches\n\n[Physical 5 / FAA Form 8020-6, printed 1 of 4, report date 10/11/01](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=5), field 11:\n\n| Field 11 row | Narrative-body time, as printed | Adjacent DATE/TIME, as printed | Arithmetic on the printed HHMM values |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Report just prior to accident | `0751 UTC` | `09/11/01 1151 UTC` | Adjacent minus body = 240 minutes |\n| First report subsequent to accident | `0951 UTC` | `09/11/01 1351 UTC` | Adjacent minus body = 240 minutes |\n\nBoth pairs explicitly use UTC labels. The separate conditions-at-accident narrative also says `0851 UTC`, but it has no matching adjacent DATE/TIME box in this comparison; no third pair is manufactured. We preserve all printed values and do not choose a preferred one, relabel a value as local time, or apply a four-hour correction to the accident, SISO, radar or recorder timelines.\n\n**Untested hypotheses:** a time-convention or export/editorial error, or another preparation error, could produce these labels. Their likelihood and specific cause are not established. The original weather product, form-preparation worksheets, native version history and applicable time instructions could discriminate. Technical weather decoding and comparison of the weather products elsewhere in the packet were not performed here. The disagreement warrants a source-quality check; by itself it shows neither a physical four-hour offset nor deliberate falsification.\n\n## A concrete historical custodian lead\n\n[Physical 16, Certified Index dated September 19, 2001](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/4-UAL-175/e-Other/4-AEA-1130-COMPLETED-ACCID-PCKG--FORMAL-ACCID-PCKG-ZNY-ARTCC-148-UAL175-REDACT-SEALED.pdf#page=16) records the Boston TRACON Air Traffic Manager's certification that listed originals were on file in that office. It lists Form 7230-4; Form 7230-10; Form 8020-6-1; Sign On Log: A90; Personnel Statements; **Original Voice Recording(s)**; and **Certified Cassette Recording(s)**.\n\nThe list distinguishes original voice recordings from certified cassette copies and identifies a historical office. It supplies a specific records/custody target. It does not authenticate today's scan signatures, prove the originals survive today, identify all recorded channels, or show that those audio originals are embedded in this PDF. Boston TRACON's contribution also must not be treated as the same facility/report version as the New York ARTCC October 11 form. No holder was contacted and no request was sent.\n\n## Consequences, missing inputs and unchanged limits\n\nThis batch advances the operational source map: previously sought SISO-style position rows are now located in the held scan; four predetermined fields can be compared; the direct statement-to-SISO instruction constrains independence; roster/overtime context qualifies an absence inference; and a labelled weather inconsistency and historical original/copy custodian list are pinned.\n\nThe native SISO export, date/timezone/code dictionary, generation/audit/correction history, roster/extra-duty definitions, original voice/cassette provenance, and independent clock anchors remain missing. No physical-clock alignment, speech/event validation, contemporaneous knowledge inference, or historical-cause determination has been performed. A shared origin could explain document agreement without proving operational accuracy; the source chain must be tested rather than counted repeatedly.\n\nThe actual May 2003 corrected QFR and delivery acknowledgment; signed action/no-action determination; and separate OIG recommendation response, implementation and closure remain unacquired. This September 2001 staffing material does not substitute for those records. All five work-plan tracks and eighteen substantive criteria remain open or partial; publication is not acceptance of them. Technical NIST/UAF, recorder/materials comparisons and independent-method audit retain their assigned owners.\n\n[Open inputs and revisit tests](CITATIONS.md#nov04-4445e152bb3aa6885605) · [Point-by-point checklist](CITATIONS.md#nov04-92e9f20f80a8380fbe1c) · [Revision diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-bd57214575a41a78b427) · [Verifier](CITATIONS.md#nov04-924657654568f853cf9e). Earlier findings and source bytes remain unchanged. No outreach, request, fee, filing, private-source disclosure, private push or history rewrite is authorized by this report.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY 76abaaa5ebacde0c0d5cdd30da5cb4c815b11c216f2d4eac901276e7b9817920 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-ed26c86f9767f929\"></a>\n### FBI telephone sources: a reported original diskette, field definitions and the Keyton counterpart\n\n**Source unit:** `fbi-keyton-acis-originals-2026-10-31` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-40cf9351349e4554523c).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY ed26c86f9767f9296f158dd7d26a79932649ccc6ce8010d1e974314a9fdd2342 -->\n# FBI telephone sources: a reported original diskette, field definitions and the Keyton counterpart\n\nOctober 31, 2026. **TEL01-TEL04 / existing AUD016-A1,TC05-B105, TC06-B110**. Two newly preserved official FBI PDF entities improve the source trail. The most useful new retrieval target is a contemporaneous report that an agent received GTE spreadsheets on paper and on an **Excel 97 diskette**. An official-hosted Keyton form supplies a counterpart to the already reviewed mirror. These are source acquisitions, not independent proof of the calls or a new historical witness.\n\n## Sources and actual reading\n\nThe public FBI item pages exposed their download links: [September part01](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-01-of-08/view) and [part02](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-02-of-08/view). Both public GETs succeeded without login or a gate workaround. Part01 has 49 pages, 4,468,234 bytes, SHA-256 **4760de107bef3c75f4d7629f90f7a70d2c861cb3b53dcbe37a3bf0fb768f93cb**; part02 has 78 pages, 5,561,167 bytes, SHA-256 **055a7ed1294c42b976cc98a65dd0a8a80d677723fa4300f0fea38fb41590bb51**. Item sizes/page counts match the preserved entities. [SOURCES.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-9d63ef4627b89de554b2) records exact URLs, acquisition times and reused sources.\n\n**Eight distinct page images were read**, part01 physical 20/45/46 and part02 physical 24/56/69/70/71. A higher-resolution crop checked one digit on already selected 46. Ordinary text from 127 pages was used only for limited locators. No whole-release reading/completeness or OCR-based absence claim follows. Raw PDFs, rasters and full extraction remain preserved outside this public payload. [Reading scope](CITATIONS.md#nov04-a163e4cf81882944ba54).\n\nThe earlier October 27 review reported an FBI bundle unacquired after 403. Its exact denied URL has not been established from the inspected history; the initial current census omitted nested publication records. Do not claim the same endpoint was never tried, that current 200 disproves the old 403, or that either access result proves concealment. Current official hosting authenticates the preserved public entity's provenance, not every assertion or complete underlying production.\n\n## 1. A concrete native-spreadsheet and attachment trail\n\n[Part01 physical 45-46](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-01-of-08/at_download/file#page=45) is a September 11, 2001 interview report, internal pages 1-2, file 265A-NY-280350. The subject is redacted; no identity is reconstructed from other releases. Page 45 says notes, a typed event summary and a screen print were handed to the interviewing agent. It attributes the UA93/UA175 spreadsheet preparation to Network Operations using **GTE ACIS billing-system information**, with relevant flights extracted/listed. The source uses cellular-call wording alongside Airphone; that wording does not establish personal-handset use.\n\nPage 46's **last paragraph** says the agent received paper copies and a copy on a **3.5-inch diskette under Microsoft Excel 97**. The diskette, native workbooks, attachment/evidence identifiers and custody are still missing. This is a precise original-record lead: obtain a preserved image/native files and the paper set, then inspect stored versus displayed dates/times, keys, sheets, formulas, flight filters and version history. Do not infer the supplied workbook was raw switch data, that its bytes survive, or that this record is the missing September 20 usage analysis.\n\n## 2. Field explanations advance the dictionary trail, with explicit limits\n\nPage 45 describes **ANSWER_STATUS 0 = no answer, 1 = answered**. Page 46 describes termination type 0 = normal / 1 = abnormal; termination direction **0 = air, 1 = ground**; reason code 0 = normal, 22 = invalid data, 24 = credit-card denied; and SCP duration as call length in seconds. Its unit/terminal/cellsite/handoff explanations give further configuration targets, not authenticated seat/caller identities.\n\nI initially misread the air-side direction digit as 1 and announced a 1/1 conflict. The closer source-image check shows 0/1. **Do not count that preliminary analyst error as an FBI discrepancy.** [Reading correction](CITATIONS.md#nov04-30121ab0e9ee0a7da614) preserves the false preliminary claim and actual validation, without rewriting the source or previous reports.\n\nThe CALL_DIAL_TIME explanation on page 45 is qualified: times **“are believed”** to use the originating caller's time zone, described as **“eastern standard time.”** Preserve that wording; no EST/EDT conversion, calibrated offset or individual-row timezone is established here. A proposed loss-of-signal/turn/range explanation for abnormal termination in the report is a provider account of possibilities, not a determination of why any selected call ended.\n\nThe previously acquired [May 13, 2004 UA175 briefing, pages 2-3](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/legislative/rg-148/2524787/t-0148-911MFR-00216.pdf#page=2) already supplies field explanations: Connect is setup time in seconds; SCP duration is time connected to the dialed number; operator duration is time on line, with no separate unit in that bullet. It describes names known in United's system and later DOJ additions from interviews. The 2001 report refers to names already in its billing system. These are differently scoped descriptions to preserve and test against native versions, not established incompatible measurements or proof of fabricated names. [Field comparison](CITATIONS.md#nov04-a6c1a7f672565a438ec2).\n\nThe 2004 briefing's Eastern/two-UA93-Central-zone account remains beside the 2001 qualified standard-time wording. No common-row identity or clock correction is inferred. An interview-based field explanation is useful evidence, but does not authenticate installed vendor/version rules, timeout/answer supervision, row allocation, billing legs or real conversations. None of these GTE rules is automatically applied to AA77's separate AT&T/Claircom records. Earlier 35-row counts/long durations/uncertain glyphs are not recalculated or resolved here.\n\n## 3. Keyton: official-hosted counterpart, dates and unresolved collect/direct accounts\n\n[Part02 physical 69](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-02-of-08/at_download/file#page=69) records Lori Lynn Keyton's account. The form prints **transcription 09/14/2001** and **investigation 9/11/01**; its dictated-date field is visibly blank. Those are distinct form fields, not automatically inconsistent event dates. About 9:00 a.m. has no printed timezone. It reports approximately 6-8 automated collect attempts, a live-operator collect call, then a later call described as **direct, not collect**, a few to five minutes later. She had **no caller ID** and did **not know onboard phone versus cellphone**. Olson/Olsen spelling variants remain as printed, without inference about identity or cause.\n\nThe [earlier mirror physical 48](https://www.les-crises.fr/wp-content/uploads/2016/06/Team7-Box13-Flight-Call-Notes-and-302s.pdf#page=48) already supplied that account. The gain is official-hosted provenance, not another independent witness. The prior review of [Voss, mirror physical 51](https://www.les-crises.fr/wp-content/uploads/2016/06/Team7-Box13-Flight-Call-Notes-and-302s.pdf#page=51) records her belief both calls were collect. Keep **Voss: believed both collect / Keyton: later direct, not collect** unresolved. Shared-office retellings are not independent network evidence.\n\nThe [Commission staff report physical 94-95, notes 266-268](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/staff-report-sept2005.pdf#page=94) retains anonymous citation dates September 14 and September 4 and a watch-log lead. The new form's dates do **not** identify an unnamed/redacted witness, repair the September 4 citation, or authenticate the unacquired watch log/9:33 clock. The quoted command center in Keyton's account is not automatically the same entity as the interview-contact route or the missing watch entry. Native office/operator/transfer records remain needed for any source-to-call/exhibit join.\n\n## 4. A separate May-family lead and duplicate-source cautions\n\n[Part02 physical 70-71](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-02-of-08/at_download/file#page=70) is a separate September 12 Las Vegas-to-Dallas communication referencing **265D-NY-280350 Serial 79**. It reports a family account of flight-attendant Renee May's **6:13 a.m.** call, with no printed timezone, and a reported **six** hijackers. Neither the time/zone nor the actual count is independently established here. Do not join it to a carrier row or another witness's call by a desired conversion.\n\nPage 71 states: **“No further investigation by Las Vegas at this time, this lead should be considered covered.”** That is the scope of this local lead, not the end of the FBI case, all telephone analysis, or an FAA administrative-action decision. Case-title bombing labels are case headings, not forensic explosives findings. Serial 79, AA receiving records, carrier evidence and the actual disposition trail remain TODOs; nothing here establishes concealment or coordination.\n\nPart01 physical 20 is another Jefferson first page; part02 physical 24/56 are similar Woodward first pages with different redaction/footers. Their continuations 21/25/57 were not visually read. Do not count the reproductions as independent corroboration, claim full-document byte/content equivalence, or use one release to fill another's redactions. Three context pages and their similarities are source-dependence cautions, not additional wrongdoing evidence.\n\n## What is still open\n\nThe next discriminating records are the reported original diskette/workbooks and paper attachments, installed ACIS field/clock rules, operator notes/logs and source-to-worksheet/exhibit joins. Keyton notes/audio/office records, the actual September 20 analysis, complete request/production chain, zero-duration exhibit provenance and Serial 79 remain TODOs. We have advanced TEL02/TEL03/TEL04 source provenance without closing any of the original 18 requirements.\n\nFAA correction/delivery/signed-action and recommendation-disposition originals remain open. The technical owner retains recorder/materials native-input and empirical tests. Steel preservation/custody/attribution and contractor-original records remain open. Publication and source hash checks are not historical validation, independent audit acceptance or substantive goal completion. [Open inputs](CITATIONS.md#nov04-dee472a04428afe915e9), [source/page/paragraph pins](CITATIONS.md#nov04-2a8d36fa28b7aa408adc), [request checklist](CITATIONS.md#nov04-35cb375e1668547b48e9), [actual additive text diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-db3ce06bddc177cf615e) and [verification](CITATIONS.md#nov04-3ca4db9da6cb9a41bbd3).\n<!-- END REPORT BODY ed26c86f9767f9296f158dd7d26a79932649ccc6ce8010d1e974314a9fdd2342 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-f6471a7744b99171\"></a>\n### A September 15 follow-up recording and an FBI 1A synopsis pointer\n\n**Source unit:** `fbi-operator-followup-tape-2026-10-31` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-85f3990bb9f9c9855467).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY f6471a7744b99171907076af4c3affbc2ecff16f436bfeef49214c9c9743b3b2 -->\n# A September 15 follow-up recording and an FBI 1A synopsis pointer\n\nOctober 31, 2026. **TEL01-TEL04 / existing AUD016-A1, TC05-B105, TC06-B110.** A newly read part of an already preserved FBI release identifies two more specific original-record targets: a **synopsis referenced in the case's 1A section** and a **taped September 15 conversation whose copy was promised to the FBI**. The actual synopsis, recording and receipt are not recovered here. This must not be described as a recording of the September 11 in-flight call.\n\n## Exact source and scope\n\n[FBI September part01, physical pages 47-49](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-01-of-08/at_download/file#page=47), item [page](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-01-of-08/view), is the same official-hosted released entity acquired in the preceding study: **49 pages, 4,468,234 bytes**, SHA-256 **4760de107bef3c75f4d7629f90f7a70d2c861cb3b53dcbe37a3bf0fb768f93cb**. Access was October 31, 2026 at 22:04:22.990490 UTC. It is a public released scan, not a native recording, evidence receipt or independent authentication of the interview statements.\n\nThree previously unread complete source images were inspected, physical 47,48,49, at 140 dpi. The adjacent ACIS report ends on 46; 47-48 are a separate FD-302 and 49 is a release information sheet. This was not a new download, new OCR, full 49-page reading, audio playback or hidden-layer examination. [Scope](CITATIONS.md#nov04-ba15127cb8c992af9e1e), [source/page/paragraph pins](CITATIONS.md#nov04-c390a4448b7829ea54ad), [source relationships](CITATIONS.md#nov04-5abb0854d2dfa43e0de8).\n\n## 1. The later recording is a distinct evidence object\n\nPhysical47, internal page1, prints **transcription 09/22/2001**, **investigation 9/22/01** and file **265A-NY-280350-NK**. The interview subject, operator and other identifying fields are redacted. The first substantive account says the subject did **not** speak with Todd Beamer during the hijacking. Do not turn this interviewee into another independently observed in-flight recipient or reconstruct redacted names from related releases.\n\nIn body paragraph 2, the report describes a UAL contact on or about **9/14/01**, provision of a **synopsis**, and a copy of that synopsis **in 1A**. It then describes a conversation arranged for **9/15/01**, says it was **taped**, and records an agreement to provide the FBI a copy **at a later date**. These are concrete retrieval anchors for the actual synopsis and recording/custody trail.\n\nKeep four objects separate: the September 11 passenger/operator conversation; the written synopsis; the September 15 follow-up conversation and its recording; and the September 22 FBI interview report. A recording of the follow-up could test what was recounted then, including later changes or dependencies. It would not automatically authenticate every statement about September 11, establish what the passenger actually said, or replace the original network/clock/recipient evidence.\n\n**Promise is not receipt.** The inspected pages do not supply the promised recording, an evidence-item/barcode, a native file hash, a signed delivery record, or a confirmed disposition. A 1A reference is a file-location lead, not proof that we possess the attachment or that it survives. Failure to recover it here is not proof it never existed, was withheld or was destroyed.\n\n## 2. Preserve the time and attribution limits\n\nPhysical47 relays an operator-conversation time of **8:45 a.m. central standard time**. Physical48 includes an attributed screaming/silence account **about 9:00 a.m.** and staying on the telephone until learning of the crash. Preserve the source wording, qualifications and redactions. The dates of the later contacts and report do not calibrate either time or establish a native call leg. No EST/EDT/Central conversion, fitted clock offset or exact interval is measured here.\n\nThe [earlier operator-source study](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/29ee78c52ae09a2cadeb33b0856c654c51982a3c/investigation/broader-evidence/fbi-vault-operator-record-2026-10-31/README.md) distinguishes a conversation, silent open connection and later indirect contact. Keep those phases and their estimates beside this later relayed account. Do not use common wording or a similar time to identify the redacted operator, declare source independence, align a carrier row or select a controlling chronology.\n\nThe strongest objection to treating a similar narrative as corroboration is that the report itself describes a **synopsis and later operator conversation** in its information chain. Similarities may reflect dependence on the same account. The actual synopsis, follow-up recording, original notes and interview histories are the discriminating records. No finding of coaching, fabrication, concealment, attack participation or motive follows from the existence of a follow-up conversation.\n\n## 3. The release's duplicate-page statement needs its own map\n\nPhysical49 is an FBI **FOIPA Deleted Page Information Sheet**. It reports **10** deleted pages and lists page labels **47,48,51,52,53,54,55,56,59,60**, each with reason **Duplicate**. The ten listed entries match the reported count. This is accounting of the printed release sheet, not independent validation of each duplicate determination.\n\nThese are the sheet's **release-page labels**. They are not established as physical PDF page numbers: physical 47-48 contain the retained interview just read, and the PDF has 49 physical pages. No original-source pagination/duplicate-to-retained-page crosswalk was acquired. Preserve both numbering systems without assuming their mapping, correcting either, or alleging an inconsistency from their numerical overlap. Neither the total nor the largest label proves the original document's length or which specific record was removed.\n\nThe sheet records a stated release-processing reason. It is not a destruction memorandum, custody disposition, evidence of deliberate removal from an archive, or a conclusion about the separate diskette/recording. Obtain the original release/page/duplicate crosswalk before interpreting completeness or treating removed duplicates as additional independent evidence.\n\n## What advances and what remains open\n\nThe preceding [ACIS/Keyton report](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/fb0d56eeaa07cfc9348306d1cedbcbea059fc5c8/investigation/broader-evidence/fbi-keyton-acis-originals-2026-10-31/README.md) identified the paper/Excel 97 diskette and operator-attachment targets. The subsequent structural check of both official PDFs found no standard embedded attachments in the inspected indexed dictionaries; that result does not establish the fate or availability of separately held original media. No native workbook or recording was recovered in this reading either.\n\nThe new advance is narrower and specific: **the September 15 recording/copy promise, the 1A synopsis reference, the September 22 source report and the release's explicit duplicate-page labels now have inspected page/paragraph locators**. A bounded check of selected earlier telephone reports found no equivalent pointer there; this is not a claim of global historical novelty, an independent witness or an authenticated native-media acquisition.\n\n[OPEN-TODOS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-809e43bf14d13bc4ea67) carries the exact synopsis/recording/receipt/release-map targets along with every original five-track requirement. The actual May 2003 correctedQFR/delivery, signed FAA administrative outcome and distinct recommendation follow-up remain missing. Recorder/materials native inputs and tests remain with the technical owner. Steel decision/custody/native inventory/attribution/contractor delivery originals remain open. None of the original 18requirements is closed by this source reading, attachment count or publication.\n\nRaw source PDFs/images/full speech, ancillary personal/contact/financial/access/signature data and private case material are excluded from this public supplement. Earlier sources, conflicting observations, reports, shared registers/workplan and public history remain unchanged. No outreach, fee, request, filing, mailbox access, unredaction, native decoding, experiment, deletion, private push or history rewrite occurred. [Checklist](CITATIONS.md#nov04-333516c6d8c9e8ceb281), [mechanical verification and limits](CITATIONS.md#nov04-8018575ea1e006a691c2), [actual addition diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-e0ce52c0595b43e40939). Public commit/ref/file verification is saved separately; it does not establish historical truth or independent audit acceptance.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY f6471a7744b99171907076af4c3affbc2ecff16f436bfeef49214c9c9743b3b2 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-137a2a9b52a16fc9\"></a>\n### FBI-hosted operator interview: speech, open connections and indirect contacts\n\n**Source unit:** `fbi-vault-operator-record-2026-10-31` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-7072081bd0b8b175459c).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY 137a2a9b52a16fc9fd533cec8333c56d70510459e8677ac889a731b339c23f04 -->\n# FBI-hosted operator interview: speech, open connections and indirect contacts\n\nOctober 31, 2026. **An official FBI-hosted release now supplies a primary interview report with GTE operator Lisa Jefferson.** It distinguishes a reported direct passenger conversation, a silent but open connection and a later Verizon-mediated contact she could not hear directly. Those distinctions matter when testing what a call duration, connection endpoint or witness time actually describes.\n\nThis is a new agency-hosted source acquisition in this work, not a claim that the historical account was previously unknown or independently confirmed. It is **not** the missing September 20 American Airlines Airphone Usage analysis, Keyton's interview, a raw network export or an authenticated source-to-exhibit worksheet. Those targets remain open.\n\n## Source and actual coverage\n\nThe ordinary FBI Vault search for Airphone returned five titles. The [September 2001 interviews, part 08 of 08 item](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-08-of-08/view) exposed [this actual PDF](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-08-of-08/at_download/file): **21 pages, 1,985,063 bytes**, SHA-256 **`a7d3d872daf33d41c993ade5ccf2e9aa5421ffb1f4cda6164d6bb712372dde41`**. The exact-title follow-up displayed zero items; that bounded index observation does not establish that the usage analysis is absent from FBI holdings or releases. Other returned September parts 01/02 remain unexamined candidate routes.\n\nWe extracted ordinary text on 21 pages only for navigation and visually read complete physical pages 3–5. **Pages 4–5 are the selected Jefferson record**; page 3 is another report's continuation and was not attributed to her. The other 18 pages were not visually read. Source identity, rendering and scope are in `SOURCES.json` and `READING-SCOPE.json`; page/paragraph pins are in `OBSERVATION-PINS.json`.\n\nPage 4 gives a September 11, 2001 visible date and identifies her role as **GTE Airphone**; page 5 supplies the continuation name/date/page 2. The visible case field is **265D-NY-280350**. These are this record's fields, not the different M-INT/265A identifier sought for the September 20 analysis. A visible unclassified-information stamp is dated 12-14-2006; that stamp is not treated as the interview or release date. Official hosting supports the retrieved release's provenance; it does not independently verify its historical assertions or supply native interview audio.\n\n## What the report distinguishes\n\n| Phase | Source account and exact location | What remains unknown |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Reported direct conversation | [Page 4](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-08-of-08/at_download/file#page=4), body paragraphs 2–5: a caller identified himself as Todd Beamer; Jefferson reports speaking with him and hearing changing cabin activity. | Voice/caller identity, actual network event, precise aircraft-event sequence and clock are not independently authenticated here. |\n| Silence while connected | [Page 5](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-08-of-08/at_download/file#page=5), first paragraph: after the last reported exchange, she heard nothing and kept the connection open for another 20 minutes before disconnecting. | An open connection is not automatically continuing passenger speech. Its native switch/session/leg and duration definitions are missing. |\n| Later provider-mediated contact | Page 5, second paragraph: a Verizon Quality Control contact relayed information from relatives reportedly speaking with another passenger. Jefferson says she could not hear that passenger and received information second hand. | Exact routing, simultaneous legs, caller identity and which connection ended at the reported endpoint are unverified. No redacted identity or LNU surname is reconstructed. |\n| Equipment indication | Page 5, end of first paragraph: tracking equipment reportedly indicated a Pittsburgh transceiver/service area. | Actual equipment logs, field definitions, service-area bounds and clock were not acquired. This is not a pinpoint aircraft location or validated flight path. |\n\nThese are statements recorded in one FBI interview report, not raw audio, switching evidence or separate independent witnesses. The firsthand/secondhand distinction is explicit in the source and must survive later summaries. Neither the silence nor the continuing provider conversation alone establishes the aircraft's physical condition or a time of passenger speech.\n\n## Preserve the time fields and conditional arithmetic\n\nThe report gives an initial **approximately 8:45 a.m. Central** call, **seven minutes** before a described change, **another eight minutes**, and a last exchange around **9:00 a.m. Central**. It then gives **another 20 minutes** open, a callback **approximately five minutes later**, a separate **9:30 a.m. Central** callback estimate and an **approximately 9:43 a.m.** conversation/connection endpoint. The last label's clock basis is read in its paragraph context, not independently calibrated.\n\nTwo nominal calculations can be reproduced from those printed values: **8:45+7+8→9:00**, while **9:00+20+5→9:25**, beside the separately estimated **9:30** callback. `TIMING-ARITHMETIC.json` retains the inputs and conditional five-minute difference. This is not an independently measured gap: the source uses estimates and supplies no uncertainty bounds. We preserve both accounts without selecting a corrected time, assigning a cause or converting them into synchronized aircraft/network timestamps. No timezone conversion, crash-time comparison or offset fit was performed.\n\nThe next useful evidence is the operator/session and Verizon transfer record, its clock basis and the interview's underlying notes/audio. Those could show whether the statements concern different legs or different events, an estimated chronology or another explanation. None is established here.\n\n## Effect on the existing telephone investigation\n\nThis advances **TEL02/TEL04** only through an agency-hosted primary operator record and a clearer source-specific retrieval target. It does not resolve the AA77 connected/unconnected descriptions, 154/159-second values, eight-call/seven-row presentation or the 35-of-37 reconstruction. GTE terminology cannot define AT&T/Claircom records automatically. The already reviewed GTE tables also contain repeated timestamps and uncertain operator-field readings; a name, similar minute or estimated duration does not create a unique row/leg match. Existing table and decoder checks were not rerun.\n\nThe source is relevant contrary evidence to flattening every connection interval into a continuing passenger conversation. Equally, it does not prove that a particular carrier row represents the silent interval or resolve the later endpoint. Native records are needed to distinguish those possibilities. No fabrication, deliberate alteration, suppression or historical cause is established by these interview estimates.\n\nAll earlier records and conflicting values remain unchanged. `OPEN-TODOS.json` retains the original 18 requirements and previous dependencies, adding the operator/Verizon/transceiver and actual report/version targets. FAA corrected-QFR/receipt and signed disposition, native recorder/calibration/clock, materials specimen/run/measurement and steel preservation/inventory/attribution/delivery gaps remain open or partial. No original requirement closes from this acquisition.\n\nThe original PDF, separate page rasters and raw extraction remain in the verified native store. Public notes exclude raw documents/images, financial/contact/home/work information, signatures, redacted identities and private case material. `VERIFICATION.json` separates source integrity/conditional arithmetic from historical validation; `CHECKLIST.md` records outcomes; `TEXT-CHANGES.diff` preserves actual new-document additions. `EDITORIAL-REVISION.diff` retains the first draft while qualifying the form-date/stamp wording and improving spacing; no source value changes. No outreach, fee, request, filing, mailbox/private correspondence, deletion, source overwrite, private push, merge or public-history rewrite occurred. Publication and goal completion remain separate milestones.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY 137a2a9b52a16fc9fd533cec8333c56d70510459e8677ac889a731b339c23f04 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-e02e5cbadac852e1\"></a>\n### Five inconsistencies: source-role tests and new transcript endpoint checks\n\n**Source unit:** `five-inconsistency-tests-2026-10-31` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-e68a6738c7e353876f21).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY e02e5cbadac852e127c0bb4fb911c29d3770adc9506327f5d99f7313546e5150 -->\n# Five inconsistencies: source-role tests and new transcript endpoint checks\n\nOctober 31, 2026. **The strongest new result qualifies the inference of missing conversations: two actual FAA transcript terminal pages end their timed dialogue before the latest listed rerecording end labels, then continue with bare minute markers.** The original range differences remain. Exact media/channel/copy joins are still missing.\n\nThis review follows the human-authorized five-item request. It checked the [current catalogue](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/60f3d93c90374e2b50147bfb19867aceccb3ba0e/investigation/catalogue/README.md), later completed readings, source/version/failure records and actual publication receipts before new work. It does not update the catalogue concurrently. [RESULTS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-85c4e1e9d87c4cb25797) gives each proposition, previous work, genuinely new work, exact locators, classification, strongest contrary finding, named missing inputs and a falsifiable next test. [SOURCE-PINS.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-f21c404a6f179464b368) retains source URLs/access dates/hashes and actual reading scope. Earlier reports and conflicting values remain unchanged.\n\n## 1. Flight/file labels: a real mismatch, an unjoined lineage\n\nThe held AWA937/NARA7599161 two-page entity was reinspected. Its first page prints AAL11 in subject and certification, ZBW-ARTCC-148 in the addressee, January17,2002, TMU commercialline1078,1255–1316UTC and the first timed row1300:41/AD1. Its second page prints the work-file label `UAL175_R42_memo.doc` and footer date09/27/2001. A pagination/masthead placeholder is also visible. In the AEA1130/NARA7598367 packet, physical186 prints UAL175 and ZNY-ARTCC-148, while the selected date,line,range and first timedrow match. [AWA source study](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/0584407dac2190a9f9514ee157124446e60672d5/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-tmu1078-source-2026-10-30/README.md); [packet study](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/e64f8f3006879bfde9fa76f1db4b9be6b514d5af/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-completed-package-original-2026-10-30/README.md).\n\nThe later [complete1078study](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/8b787be27fcb665a851a42e318ccc4fd0b6117e9/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-1078-marked-passages-2026-10-30/README.md) already read all five pages186–190 and preserved48timedrows,45distinctsecondlabels and22minutemarkers. We reuse that completed work; the middle pages are **not** currently missing/unread in this source block. The AWA entity visibly contains one timed dialogue row followed by its administrative footer page; it is not the same complete rendered entity as the five-page block. That does not establish why its contents differ, a missing recording, or a common native origin. Selected first-row/header agreement cannot authenticate a full-document join.\n\n**Classification:** confirmed subject/file/work-file contrasts; native lineage unresolved. A ground ATC line may concern several aircraft, and subject-specific transcription/certification can select material. Template reuse, a different subject selection, assembly and version differences remain testable alternatives, not resolved causes. Needed: native Word/enclosure/version identifiers, selection instructions, actual source channel and assembly/distribution records. Neither flight label is preferred.\n\n## 2. Retention: keep the printed destruction date beside the hold\n\nAEA1130physical1 prints **DESTROY: March11,2004**. The [September14,2001instruction](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/45ee3925dc1edd3137cf85182570bfc8440afcac/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-retention-directive-2026-10-30/README.md), AEA2579physical1, says retain and secure administrative/operational data until further notice and retain when uncertain. Its temporal/facility scope and differing OIG-reported interpretations remain preserved. A label is not an executed disposal record, and the OIG investigation of another locally created tape does not establish this package's disposition.\n\nThe already-read [2005specialschedule](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/8bc80894a40ac207b2ccce62a9fdd6d22d4c1139/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-911-special-schedule-2026-10-30/README.md) separates permanent original records/audio re-recordings from temporary website-image backups, convenience/generation and additional dissemination/revision copies. Its60-day and180-day periods have different classes and triggers. It **cannot by itself establish authorization in2004 or withdrawal of the2001hold**. Original tape, re-recording, accident package, recordkeeping copy, convenience copy and surviving scan cannot silently be treated as one object.\n\n**Classification:** qualified retention tension; governing applicability and actual disposition unresolved. The surviving scan and permanent-original class are contrary evidence to a universal no-surviving-copy claim, without proving an original tape survived. Named TODOs are the applicable historical authority, copy classification, hold receipt/withdrawal/exception, custody/transfer and actual disposition records. No source, schedule or date is normalized into a finding of lawful or unlawful disposal. No deletion occurred.\n\n## 3. End labels versus actual terminal pages: a new qualification\n\nThe [Request2Item16list](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/62a102da31cc82a7216dc41e60bed185a11c8d45/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-response-labels-2026-10-30/README.md) preserves these conditional pairings. Physical15/printed8 points onward to Item20; physical16/printed9 includes the Boston entry, transcript labels and further cross-references. It is not the complete production manifest.\n\n| Conditional role/start pairing | Listed rerecording ranges | Transcript range | Last printed timed dialogue row in held counterpart | Terminal minute label |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| NewYorkOperationsManager |1230–1331;1331–1340|1230–1344|**1338:24**, newly read physical173/printed26of26|1344|\n| NewYorkTMU1245start |1245–1314|1245–1316|**1310:37**, newly read physical185/printed9of9|1316|\n| NewYorkTMU1255start/1078candidate |1255–1315|1255–1316|**1310:14**, prior completed block physical190, ledgerP190R05|1316|\n| BostonMilitaryOperationsSpecialist |1232–1259|1232–1259|Not examined here|Matching declared1259label|\n\nPhysical173 has bare markers1339–1344 and EndofTranscript. Physical185 has bare1311–1316markers and EndofTranscript; its top case header is partly occluded, and the visibleUAL175/agency/page fields and prior cover mapping are retained without reconstructing covered characters. The existing1078ledger retains its final row and following markers. The two endpoint candidates were frozen before viewing; their actual printed labels confirmed these selected terminal-page mappings. Other middle pages remain unread. Our checked lineage now covers20distinctAEA1130pageimages, leaving217unread; that is not a full-package or