rical authentication. `CHECKLIST.md` records requirement outcomes; `TEXT-CHANGES.diff` is the actual new-document Git addition diff. `EDITORIAL-REVISION.diff` preserves the final clarification of one paraphrase against the retained draft: remembered information before staff heard of a second hijack, rather than before the independently timed event. No source wording or value was changed. Public payload excludes raw marked pages/full speech, ancillary personnel/contact/access details, private correspondence, strategy and credentials. No outreach, fee, request, filing, source repair/deletion, audio decoding, clock fitting, experiment, private push or history rewrite occurred. Publication is a separate verified milestone, not acceptance of the substantive goal.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY af56e6e51af941eb413af65931db4efe2f77760b7bdc56ce493b9436b03910e5 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-5641efa31d50b8c0\"></a>\n### The purportedly omitted call: what two candidate copies actually contain\n\n**Source unit:** `neads-omitted-call-candidates-2026-10-31` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-017d1b29f784370fa76d).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY 5641efa31d50b8c0f237f1e4068ec62d3340d1f8e7b02dca36344f1d0a6413a6 -->\n# The purportedly omitted call: what two candidate copies actually contain\n\nOctober 31, 2026. **The Alderson-attributed public candidate contains a near-matching difficult-time/military-coordinator call and the following conditional Langley-backup discussion.** That is a substantive qualification to a broad omission reading of the staff notes. It does not settle which versions or exact call the notes meant, whether a whole clear call was retained, or whether timing errors were corrected.\n\nThe [official interview/review notes, page 17](https://www.archives.gov/files/declassification/iscap/pdf/2011-048-doc8.pdf#page=17), report that a clear ZNY positioning call and some following conversations occur in an original transcript but not Alderson, and require a later MCC Channel 2 timing redo. That warning remains preserved, unchanged and unresolved. The exact versions, source/channel relationship and completed verification remain TODOs.\n\n## Positive passages, not an absence inferred from a search\n\n| Selected passage | Earlier [compiled reproduction](https://www.scribd.com/document/14142009/NYC-Box-2-NEADS-Transcript-Rome-NY-Fdr-Transcript-Pgs-1-82-1237-1417) | [Alderson-attributed candidate](https://www.scribd.com/document/14142047/NYC-Box-3-Neads-conr-norad-Fdr-Transcript-Neads-Channel-2-Mcc-Upside-006) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Difficulty contacting the military coordinator; NORAD wants fighters held over New York | Printed59/NCT0000107, lower block; rows `:53`, `:54`, `:57` after the earlier `13:12:02` label | Viewer22, internal 0052, lines 8–25 |\n| Nearby short McCarthy/Nasypany exchange | Printed60/NCT0000108, `13:13:29` through `:41` | Viewer22, internal 0053, lines 5–15 |\n| Conditional Langley/tanker/backup discussion | Printed60/NCT0000108, `13:15:04` paragraph | Viewer23, internal 0055, lines 1–7 |\n\nThe content sequence gives a better comparison than a single word match. It does **not** authenticate a unique audio event, prove the note's named caller, or establish identical complete calls. These copies may share underlying recordings and are not independent witnesses. No clock offset is calculated; abbreviated old time fields remain abbreviated rather than expanded into invented fully printed timestamps. Exact locators and limits are in `COMPARISON-PINS.json`, OC01/03/05.\n\nThe strongest objection to concluding that the staff warning was wrong is that **we have not identified the exact versions or exact call it referred to**. A later/different transcript, another related call, a channel/coverage difference or partial versus complete audibility could matter. These are untested alternatives, not resolved explanations. Conversely, the currently visible content prevents treating the warning as proof that every Alderson-attributed copy lacks this entire sequence.\n\n## A new comparison confound and a local wording difference\n\n**Channel scope:** immediately above the earlier copy's target call, printed 59 has a **Channel 12/MCC-OP** heading. Its DRM number is not clearly supplied in that local heading; we do not fill it in. The previously inspected printed 50 heading was **DRM2/DAT2 Channel 2**, while the other candidate's cover identifies **DRM1/DAT2 Channel 2 MCC Upside**. All three observations remain side by side. Do not automatically assign the printed 50 heading to every later block, or treat this as a demonstrated Channel 2-to-Channel 2 comparison. The additional context does not resolve the DRM-label discrepancy or prove why the note reports an omission. OC02 records the legible local fields, the separate reader rendering and the punctuation/DRM-digit uncertainty beside the prior labels.\n\n**Tanker wording:** old printed 60, the `:37` sentence, displays **A KC10**; new viewer 23/internal 0054, line 16 displays **Two KC-10s**. This is a local text difference in near-matching passages. It is not evidence that one or two tankers were actually ordered or supplied. Neither reading is selected. Source recordings, speaker/context mapping and transcription quality-control records would be needed to test it. OC04 preserves both.\n\n## What was actually read and preserved\n\nTwo ordinary reader finds supplied positive locators in both public copies. Actual visual reading then covered selected viewports on old printed 59/60 and new viewer 22/23. The first old59 screenshot was clipped and did not show the target call; it is retained as such. One prospectively documented extra same-page view after ordinary browser zoom exposed the lower call and footer. Old60 was zoomed out with small text and only selected passages adopted, with reader locator support. New22/23 show the relevant internal sections, but parts of those pages remain outside the view. No complete 82-page or 93-page review, whole-version diff or exhaustive following-conversation test occurred. `READING-SCOPE.json` records the actual deviation from the initial viewport cap rather than hiding it.\n\nFive unedited JPEGs remain in the verified native media store. `SOURCES.json` gives each byte count/SHA-256, including the clipped view. Key target views are old59lower SHA-256 `2be27ccbce1866ead7430d0c8343a19f4e436a155c5bc62b908945974787db07` and new22 SHA-256 `9e6680750eec50897d0d45187b1f453840ef8650a6c70c50a75551e86bdc0be2`. These identify **our browser representations**, not native transcript PDFs or historical masters. No native source PDF was acquired or hashed. The official note packet's separately retained hash and previous source readings are distinguished from this unit's preview work.\n\nThe previews displayed no reading gate for these views. No signup, download, trial, ad installation, hidden endpoint, source repair or gate workaround was used. Platform AI titles/upload fields and handwritten annotations are not adopted as authentication. Raw screenshots/marked pages/full speech and incidental personal/contact/access/ad details are excluded from the public notes.\n\n## What remains to obtain\n\nObtain the actual original/Alderson version pair, source/copy/channel manifests and staff call/page referents; then test the whole allegedly missing call and the specific following conversations. Obtain the post-about9:10 redo worksheets, validated output and delivery history. A completed and propagated correction could weaken an integrity concern; an established known uncorrected error in the actual relied-on version could strengthen a narrower one. These candidates establish neither outcome.\n\n`OPEN-TODOS.json` preserves the prior MC/MFR/AV/OM dependencies and original 18 requirements. AV-T02 advances only to **candidate passages inspected**. FAA corrected-QFR/receipt and signed action/no-action/recommendation outcomes, telephone native field/call joins, recorder configuration/custody/clock inputs, materials specimen/native-calibration joins and steel preservation/inventory/attribution/delivery gaps remain open/partial. No calibrated technical-owner test was duplicated. No original criterion is complete from this comparison.\n\nThe [preceding report](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/e7e67b0b3ced08a95400a401448dee6dc4ae3a4e/investigation/broader-evidence/neads-alderson-preview-2026-10-31/README.md), conflicting records and all earlier source bytes remain unchanged. `CHECKLIST.md` accounts for the requirements; `SOURCE-CHECKS.json` separates representation checks from historical validation; `TEXT-CHANGES.diff` supplies actual additive text changes. `EDITORIAL-REVISION.diff` preserves the pre-publication qualification of hard-to-read heading punctuation against the retained draft; this changes our precision, not the source. No outreach, fee, request, filing, private-case use, deletion, private push, merge or public-history rewrite occurred. Publication is a separate milestone, not substantive goal completion.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY 5641efa31d50b8c0f237f1e4068ec62d3340d1f8e7b02dca36344f1d0a6413a6 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-f7deb2f5cb7549a4\"></a>\n### Port Authority support letter, report use and the missing confirmation\n\n**Source unit:** `steel-cooperation-records-2026-11-01` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-1d6f37d1b83091734c96).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY f7deb2f5cb7549a41955380bec065e0823ed988e9cd100f623e758f073f38694 -->\n# Port Authority support letter, report use and the missing confirmation\n\nNovember 1, 2026. Existing B188/H005, AUD011/AUD016, SPT01–SPT08 and SR01. This adds an underlying published letter reproduction to the steel preservation/access inquiry; it does not supply the executed agreement described in earlier testimony.\n\n## The newly read record\n\nThe Port Authority's letter to NIST director Arden Bement is dated **January 24, 2002**. Its reproduced office stamp reads **2002 JAN 28 P 2:13**. These are separate fields; neither authenticates historical dispatch, receipt or a calibrated clock. The full image identifies Francis J. Lombard as chief engineer. [Hearing, printed 176](https://books.google.com/books?id=R1GrCiT1US8C&pg=PA176); source PA176 in [SOURCES.json](CITATIONS.md#nov04-11a20c4d23ab31a25053).\n\nThe letter supports the proposed investigation, describes staff attending the January 10 briefing, and states an understanding restricting report use in damages suits and investigators' expert testimony. It asks NIST to confirm that stated understanding. This is a specific confirmation-request lead, not a recovered NIST acceptance or completed bilateral agreement.\n\nThe underlying image and public text both use **submitted** in the report-use clause. The historical statute below uses **admitted**. Both records remain unchanged; no legal effect or cause of that wording difference is decided here.\n\n## Necessary statutory context\n\nThe **2000 edition of 15 U.S.C. §281a** already limited use of resulting investigation reports in damages suits. That matters when assessing whether the report-use language was exceptional. The provision's inspected paragraph contains no separate investigator expert-testimony prohibition; this limited textual observation does not establish that no other rule, agreement or advice addressed testimony. [Official historical text, physical 1 / printed 546](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2000-title15/pdf/USCODE-2000-title15-chap7-sec281a.pdf); source USC2000, extraction lines 111–124.\n\nThe letter, statute and earlier witness descriptions must be kept separate. No conclusion about enforceability, unlawful restrictions, deliberate concealment or what NIST actually accepted follows from this comparison. The strongest qualification is that the language may reflect existing law or additional guidance not yet acquired.\n\n## Other cooperation records and contrary evidence\n\nThe public reader's text groups reproduce FEMA support, NYC Emergency Management's February 22, 2002 letter, and FDNY and city Design and Construction statements about consultation and proposed cooperation. Their accounts add concrete records to the earlier hearing summary. They do not establish that every requested drawing, artifact or access permission was delivered. Full facsimile review of those other letters remains pending; omitted image headers and transcription defects are explicitly retained. [Reader group 161](https://books.google.com/books?id=R1GrCiT1US8C&pg=PA161&output=text), [group 171](https://books.google.com/books?id=R1GrCiT1US8C&pg=PA171&output=text); B161/B171.\n\nThese prospective statements qualify any blanket account of permanent total refusal. They do not erase earlier access difficulties, documented processing losses, or the missing item-specific custody and attribution records. They are participants' accounts of the coordinated effort, not independent proof of every operational step.\n\n## What was actually acquired and read\n\nA complete displayed image of printed 176 was downloaded and preserved, with all substantive paragraphs visually checked. Four public plain-text reader groups starting at 161, 166, 171 and 176 were examined; embedded image-only material was not thereby read. One official historical-code PDF was acquired and its relevant paragraph checked against a full-page rendering. This is not a complete facsimile reading of the book or authentication of native correspondence.\n\nThe earlier official hearing HTML contains image filenames rather than the letter bodies. That observation remains correct for that representation; it is not proof that the letters disappeared. The complete-book API/download attempts remain unsuccessful and preserved separately. The new image/text readings do not convert those failures into historical evidence. HTML navigation markers must not silently become printed-PDF page references.\n\n[Open records](CITATIONS.md#nov04-1e9b9c2e9f3698a2a5e2) identifies the reply, routing, authority and earlier-agreement joins still needed. A separate [audio method note](CITATIONS.md#nov04-2f10714dd05f053db075) records the recovered local control outputs and why they cannot adjudicate the aircraft descriptions. These are distinct source chains.\n\n[Checklist](CITATIONS.md#nov04-af3d99af34c6aab17363), [verification script](CITATIONS.md#nov04-709ead702b36ac19cf16), [verification scope/results](CITATIONS.md#nov04-c27dce7f8dd71471c55a) and [additive text diff](CITATIONS.md#nov04-d9f41d66c1bc224ac034) accompany this supplement. Prior reports and conflicting records remain unchanged. All five work-plan tracks and eighteen substantive requirements remain open or partial; publication does not complete them.\n<!-- END REPORT BODY f7deb2f5cb7549a41955380bec065e0823ed988e9cd100f623e758f073f38694 -->\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"supplement-9d168a068410648b\"></a>\n### Steel provenance: exhibition folders and a photographed label\n\n**Source unit:** `steel-exhibition-records-and-label-2026-11-03` · [unchanged original body](CITATIONS.md#nov04-7a89811bcb425088b337).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full owning report, including limits, failed checks and TODOs</summary>\n\n<!-- BEGIN REPORT BODY 9d168a068410648be7cb21b291ba85b20f2249c7cf243c1a911f69f04556cc7e -->\n# Steel provenance: exhibition folders and a photographed label\n\n**November 3, 2026.** A newly acquired institutional finding aid supplies two exact archive-folder targets. The folders themselves remain unacquired. A separately preserved public museum photograph did not yield a reliably readable accession number or label transcript, even at its offered original size. Its photographer-written caption adds another unverified attribution, which remains separate from museum records.\n\nThis advances the operational steel-record search within **STL01–STL03, AUD011/AUD016, B188/H005 and SPT01–SPT08**. It does not complete the original preservation, attribution, transfer or testing criteria. Beverly Hills certification and approved attachments remain TODOs; their failed access routes were not retried.\n\n## Two precise archive targets\n\nThe [New-York Historical Society Museum Department finding aid](https://findingaids.library.nyu.edu/nyhs/nyhs_archives_rg20/all/) identifies collection **NYHS-RG 20**, **Series II. Exhibitions / Subseries II.A. General Files**. NYU hosts the finding aid; New York Historical holds the described archival collection. Neither is the New York State Museum in Albany.\n\n| Catalog entry | Catalog date | Container | Exact saved-HTML locator |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Recovery: The World Trade Center Recovery Operation at Fresh Kills | 2003 | **Offsite-Box 220, Folder 8** | `aspace_ref1945_9j7`, opening tag line 6272; title line 6274 |\n| World Trade Center Monument (Research File) | circa 2002 | **Offsite-Box 218, Folder 24** | `aspace_ref1915_gke`, opening tag line 5632; title line 5634 |\n\nThe parent description says exhibition files vary substantially. Possible contents include object lists, loans, labels and travel documentation, but those contents are **not confirmed for either folder**. Catalog years generally denote exhibition openings; folder documents can have other dates. Catalog titles may differ from physical folder headings. Most files require advance retrieval from offsite storage. These statements describe the catalog, not a completed access request or inspection. The collection-level restrictions on some other records do not establish that these particular folders are restricted—or that access is guaranteed.\n\nThese are documentary leads, not a steel-specimen identification. The exhibition-title association makes Folder 8 worth checking for loan/object documentation; it does not establish that it includes the Albany permanent columns or the previously referenced visual inventory. The generic monument title does not establish which monument, object, institution or metal it concerns. Neither entry links a digital folder body in the acquired finding aid.\n\n## Photograph: caption and label are different evidence\n\nThe public [WTC steel-column photograph](https://www.flickr.com/photos/wallyg/55558340145/), attributed on its page to **Wally Gobetz / wallyg**, was actually viewed at **683×1024** and at the offered **4000×6000** size. These are two representations of one photograph, not two witnesses or independent views. The site labels the photograph as taken August 9, 2026; that field is not an independently authenticated capture time.\n\nThe caption attributes the displayed double-column fragment to the **North Tower**, describes a **36-foot, three-column assembly originally weighing 20 tons**, and places it on the **west facade, ninth column from the southwest corner, floors 104–107**. These are the photographer's assertions. The caption's source and method of attribution were not supplied or authenticated in this review. Its assembly dimensions are not an analyst's measurement of the surviving fragment.\n\nTwo faithful, native-pixel detail crops of the central plinth label and lower-right card were read. Their text is insufficiently legible for a reliable transcript, accession number or member mark. **No label wording was reconstructed from the caption, enhanced by AI, or treated as read.** No material composition, fracture, damage-mechanism or temperature test was performed. The photograph and crops remain local; the public derivative contains no image or cropped-image redistribution. The [offered-size page](https://www.flickr.com/photos/wallyg/55558340145/sizes/o/) identifies the license as [CC BY-NC-ND 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).\n\n## Keep the descriptions unjoined\n\n| Source role | Preserved attribution | Context and unresolved join |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Museum-authored [phase-two release](https://nysm.nysed.gov/about/press/phase-2-world-trade-center-exhibition-opens-feb-1-nys-museum), captured previously | **North / floors 71-74 / 20-foot / 10-ton** | Recovery; live page carries a January 1, 2003 date field, not authenticated native posting history. |\n| Museum-authored [exhibition page](https://nysm.nysed.gov/exhibitions/ongoing/world-trade-center-rescue-recovery-response), captured previously | **South / floors 7–9** | Rescue; no accession join established in the read paragraph. |\n| Photographer caption, preserved in this unit | **North / floors 104–107 / 36-foot / 20 tons** | Description of original assembly; no independently read label or object-ID join. |\n\nThese values stay side by side without choosing a preferred account. They do **not** establish a contradiction about one physical specimen. Different objects/displays, a loan or replacement, a changed attribution, a descriptive error, and a caption error remain untested possibilities. The strongest objection to a misidentification claim is that neither an identical object nor equivalent measurement scope has been established. Do not count a caption repeating a label as independent corroboration; whether this caption did so remains unknown.\n\nAn accession-to-label-to-photograph crosswalk, original identification/marking records and installation/loan history could distinguish the possibilities. No link to **I-0137, W14A/W14B, C12a/b, FEMA Sample 1, a NIST coupon or WTC 7** was established. None of these source observations establishes fabrication, suppression, improper disposal, motive or a collapse mechanism.\n\n## Source pins and reading scope\n\n| Preserved representation | Acquisition or preservation UTC | Bytes | SHA-256 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Complete institutional finding-aid HTML | 2026-11-03T17:47:49.752747+00:00 | 1,364,396 | `44897609eddc23f08ffbf3b94c3b3dea163b02e02d897ed19454a4d3dc0972bc` |\n| Displayed photograph | 2026-11-03T17:57:09.971553+00:00, local preservation | 98,495 | `aca21bc0c510634157ec146749c17b0593bdf2242c194127aba31e16f03cdd72` |\n| Offered original-size photograph | 2026-11-03T17:57:09.972019+00:00, request start | 4,716,712 | `2b240561df1ba32d024f8d0669e24c5f8d418d247f1a2534aac91e483256562b` |\n| Photograph-page browser DOM snapshot | 2026-11-03T18:02:28.217536+00:00, saved-file timestamp | 17,389 | `fe652637303abbd2ecda8d2801ce6320988a7737ae190b402720d051c0169253` |\n| Offered-size-page browser DOM snapshot | 2026-11-03T18:02:06.799531+00:00, saved-file timestamp | 4,867 | `22c61246a836146aaf72093b87c8ab9a65e99ae6f36b03c58772655064b1f041` |\n\nFinding-aid entries, collection/series access context and the relevant parent notes were read; the entire collection's described documents were not. Saved HTML lines **794, 804 and 813** locate the parent scope, arrangement and access-note blocks. The caption is at saved browser-DOM line **204**; the taken-date field at **212**; offered size at its separate snapshot line **47**. These are representation-specific locators, not original physical-folder or label pagination. The downloaded HTML and image entity hashes are not original historical records, compressed wire captures or object authentication. Browser-DOM hashes identify that captured representation only.\n\nFour focused queries were used in this bounded unit. The last exact museum/floor-number query yielded no pertinent primary object record among the displayed results; unrelated returns were not adopted. No global absence or archive-exhaustion finding follows. No denied museum/PDF, Beverly Hills or Smithsonian route was repeated. No agency/archive/photographer contact, fee, request, login or physical acquisition occurred.\n\n## Outstanding inputs and preserved versions\n\n**NYSM-05:** Obtain Folder 8's actual contents, including any object list, lender/loan receipt, captions, media inventory and exhibition installation records. Check actual